  { id:'opqb-t10-410', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'All of the following are part of **uvea** **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Pars plicata','Pars plana','Choroid',"Schwalbe's line"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The uvea is three structures and only three, and the question is built so that two of the four options are halves of the same one.**\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 3 is the whole answer, printed as a three-item list with one sub-list:\n\n| Uveal tract | |\n|---|---|\n| **Iris** | |\n| **Ciliary body** | → **Pars plicata** · **Pars plana** |\n| **Choroid** | |\n\nThe book says the same in prose: *\"The uveal tract (the uvea) is the **vascular middle layer of the eye**. It is divided into three parts: the iris, the ciliary body and the choroid arranged from front to back\"* (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.131), and it divides the ciliary body into *\"1. The pars plicata anteriorly 2. The pars plana posteriorly\"* (p.132).\n\nSo **A and B are both the ciliary body** — the folded, secreting front part and the flat, quiet back part — and **C is the choroid**. Three of the four options are uvea.\n\n**Why Schwalbe's line is not.** It is a **corneal** landmark, not a uveal one: the termination of Descemet's membrane, forming the anterior boundary of the trabecular meshwork in the drainage angle. It belongs to the angle structures seen on gonioscopy, not to the vascular middle coat. *(The definition of Schwalbe's line is **not taken from the course material** — grepped case-insensitively across all 29 lecture caches and all 12 cached book chapters, \"Schwalbe\" returns no teaching text. What the module does print is the neighbouring relationship: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.131, *\"In front of the root of the iris lies the **trabecular meshwork**, through which the aqueous humour drains out of the eye\"*.)*\n\n**The distinction worth carrying:** everything that inflames in \"uveitis\" is on the list of three; the angle landmarks that a gonioscopist names are not.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The uvea is the iris, ciliary body (pars plicata and pars plana) and choroid; Schwalbe’s line is a corneal/angle landmark, not a uveal structure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q410), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-411', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'In **anterior uveitis** the pupil is generally:',
    options:['Of normal size','Miotic, irregular and poorly reactive','Miotic, irregular and reactive','Mid-dilated regular reactive'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three properties, and the bank wants all three right: small, misshapen, and sluggish.**\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 17 prints the pupil sign as two bullets:\n\n> **Pupil** — *Miosis with sluggish pupillary light reaction.* · *Irregular shape due to synechiae*\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134 adds the word *early*: **\"Miosis (an early sign) with sluggish pupillary light reaction.\"** — the same fact this topic keys again at Q428.\n\n**Why each property happens:**\n\n| Property | Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| **Miotic** | Reflex spasm of the iris sphincter in an inflamed iris. `L37) Red Eye`: *\"The pupil in an eye with iridocyclitis typically is somewhat **smaller** than that of the other eye, due to **reflex spasm of the iris sphincter muscle**.\"* |\n| **Irregular** | **Posterior synechiae** — inflammatory adhesions between the posterior iris and the anterior lens capsule. Where the iris is stuck it cannot move, so the pupil pulls into a festooned outline (Q436 offers *festooned pupil* as an option for exactly this reason). |\n| **Poorly reactive** | The same two things together: a spastic sphincter and an iris tethered to the lens cannot execute a brisk light reflex. |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Of normal size** | Normal size and shape belongs to **conjunctivitis** — `L37) Red Eye`: *\"Conjunctivitis does not affect the pupil.\"* |\n| **Miotic, irregular and reactive** | ⚠️ The near-miss, and it differs from the key by one word. It gets the size and the shape right and the *reaction* wrong; both sources specify a **sluggish** reaction. |\n| **Mid-dilated regular reactive** | The mid-dilated pupil is the signature of **acute angle-closure glaucoma**, and there it is **fixed**, not reactive — `L12) Glaucoma 2`: *\"Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil\"*. This topic makes that contrast the whole question at Q419. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The pupil in anterior uveitis is miotic from sphincter spasm, irregular from posterior synechiae, and sluggish — against the mid-dilated fixed pupil of acute angle closure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q411), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-412', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**Aqueous humor** is formed by:',
    options:['Epithelium of ciliary body','Posterior surface of iris','Lens','Pars plana'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Aqueous is secreted by the ciliary epithelium — specifically the non-pigmented layer covering the folds of the pars plicata.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.132 prints it in one line: **\"The non-pigmented epithelium of the pars plicata is responsible for aqueous humour secretion.\"** It lists the ciliary body's three functions on the same page: *\"1. Accommodation 2. Aqueous humour production 3. It provides an attachment to the zonular fibers keeping the lens in place.\"*\n\n⚠️ **The lecture deck never says this.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` shows the ciliary body only as the words *Pars plicata / Pars plana* on slide 3, and its own NOT-COVERED audit records the hole: *\"AQUEOUS HUMOUR production by the ciliary epithelium … its secretory function is never stated.\"* The book is the source for this question, which is the module's ruling — the book is the main reference where the deck only names a structure.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Pars plana** | ⚠️ The real trap, because it is also ciliary body. The pars plana is the flat posterior strip — the surgical entry zone and the site of **snow-banking** in intermediate uveitis (Q421, Q433). It does not secrete; the **folded** pars plicata does, and the folds exist to give the secreting epithelium surface area. |\n| **Posterior surface of iris** | The back of the iris is **pigment epithelium** — two layers of it, continuous with the ciliary epithelium embryologically, but not secretory. Aqueous *passes over* it through the posterior chamber, which is why an iris stuck to the lens (seclusio) dams the flow and bows the iris forward (Q424, Q432). |\n| **Lens** | Avascular and nourished **by** the aqueous, not the reverse. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.133: the uveal vessels *\"indirectly supply nutrients to the outer retina, sclera, and lens, which lack blood supply.\"* |\n\n**Why the answer matters clinically:** every drug that lowers intraocular pressure by reducing secretion — beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha agonists, all three offered in this topic at Q435 and Q441 — acts on this epithelium. And the same epithelium shutting down under inflammation is why the pressure in acute iridocyclitis is usually **low** (Q440, Q447).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Aqueous humour is secreted by the non-pigmented epithelium of the pars plicata of the ciliary body — not by the pars plana, the iris or the lens.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q412), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-413', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The **earliest feature** of anterior uveitis includes:',
    options:['Keratic precipitates','Hypopyon','Posterior synechiae','Aqueous flare'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Aqueous flare: composed of proteins circulating in the aqueous 2ry to inflammation and increased vascular permeability. This altered highly viscous aqueous humor is called plasmoid aqueous.\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box is the book almost word for word.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135: *\"**Aqueous flare:** composed of **proteins** circulating in the aqueous secondary to inflammation and increased vascular permeability. This altered highly viscous aqueous humour is called **plasmoid aqueous.**\"*\n\n**Why flare comes first — the question is really about a time course.** The blood–aqueous barrier is the first thing inflammation breaks. Once it leaks, **protein** enters the aqueous immediately; protein scatters a slit beam, and that scatter is *flare* (the Tyndall effect — the beam becomes visible crossing the chamber, like a torch beam in smoke). Everything else on the option list needs something more than a leak:\n\n| Option | What it needs, and how long it takes |\n|---|---|\n| **Keratic precipitates** | Cells must first enter the chamber, then **settle and clump** on the corneal endothelium. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 defines them as *\"formation of clumps of cells (WBCs) on the back surface of the cornea\"* — clumping is a later step than leaking. |\n| **Hypopyon** | Neutrophils in enough quantity to form a visible fluid level. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134: *\"**Hypopyon (pus in the AC)**: it represents a collection of neutrophils and exudates in the inferior part of the AC.\"* A sign of **severe**, not early, inflammation. |\n| **Posterior synechiae** | Fibrin must organise into an adhesion between iris and lens. Both sources list synechiae under **complications/sequelae**, not signs (`L13,14` slide 18; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135) — and Q438 of this topic keys them as the sign of **chronic, inactive** disease. |\n\n**The pairing to remember, and the two questions in this topic that turn on it:** flare and cells mean the eye is inflamed **now** (Q436 keys exactly that — *\"They indicate current uveal inflammation, not just past damage\"*), while synechiae, pigmented KPs and iris atrophy are the **scars** of inflammation that has already happened.\n\n⚠️ **Neither source grades flare.** `L13,14` slide 16 prints only *\"Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± hypopyon\"*, and the book records its own absence: no SUN scheme, no 0.5+ to 4+ scale anywhere in its twelve pages. If an exam asks for a *grade*, the module has not taught one.",
    objective:'Aqueous flare — protein leaking through a broken blood–aqueous barrier — is the earliest sign of anterior uveitis; KPs, hypopyon and synechiae all require a further step and appear later.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q413), key p.69 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-414', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'Treatment of **iridocyclitis** can include all of the following **EXCEPT**',
    options:['SYSTEMIC STEROIDS','Topical steroids','Vasodilator','Cycloplegics'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Treatment of iridocyclitis\n\n1. **Steroids (topical- periocular- systemic)** to control the inflammation and decrease the incidence of complications.\n2. Cycloplegic-mydriatic topical medications: to decrease photophobia and pain from iris and ciliary spasm.\n3. Antiglaucoma medications in case of 2ry glaucoma (avoid prostaglandin analogues)\n4. Control of the causative condition\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The printed box is the syllabus list, and both sources print it identically.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 give the same four items in the same order; the deck adds a fifth, *\"Treatment of secondary ocular complications\"*, which the book marks **Tutorial**.\n\n**So the four options map onto the list like this:** A and B are item 1 (the box's own parenthesis names topical, periocular **and** systemic routes — which is why Q442 asks which route is best); D is item 2. **Only \"vasodilator\" is on no list anywhere.**\n\n**Why a vasodilator is the wrong idea, not merely an unlisted one.** The problem in uveitis is that uveal vessels are already **too leaky** — that is what produces flare (Q413), cells and hypopyon. Dilating them further would increase, not reduce, the protein and cells crossing into the aqueous. The therapeutic direction is the opposite: steroids **stabilise** the blood–aqueous barrier and suppress the inflammatory cascade. *(That vasodilatation would worsen exudation is reasoning about the mechanism the sources teach; no source names any vasodilator in connection with uveitis — \"vasodilator\" returns nothing in the uvea deck or book chapter — so the claim is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Two details in the box worth lifting out:**\n\n- **\"avoid prostaglandin analogues\"** — the box prints the caution without a reason. Prostaglandins are inflammatory mediators, so a prostaglandin-analogue drop can aggravate uveitis and provoke cystoid macular oedema; the antiglaucoma drugs the bank does accept here are beta-blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (Q435, Q441). *(The reason is **not taken from the course material**; the instruction to avoid them is.)*\n- **Cycloplegics do two jobs, not one.** The deck spells the second one out where the box does not: *\"To prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae\"* (`L13,14` slide 20). Q420 of this topic is built entirely on that pair of rationales.\n\n**Note what the box does NOT contain:** no drug name, no dose, no frequency. The book records the same absence for its whole chapter — *\"NO DOSE, NO DROP FREQUENCY … IS PRINTED ANYWHERE IN THE TWELVE PAGES\"* — so the *atropine 1 %* and *pilocarpine 2 %* of Q418 come from the bank alone.",
    objective:'Iridocyclitis is treated with steroids, cycloplegic–mydriatics, antiglaucoma drugs if the IOP rises and treatment of the cause; a vasodilator appears on no list and would worsen exudation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q414), key p.69 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-415', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The following **investigations** are useful in the case of uveitis **EXCEPT**',
    options:['Chest x-ray','Rheumatologic assessment','Echocardiography.','Tuberculin test.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS NO SOURCE IN THE MODULE, AND THE ABSENCE IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** Grepped case-insensitively across all 29 lecture caches in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and all 12 cached book chapters in `content\\ophtho\\book\\`: **\"echocardiograph\" returns 0 hits anywhere in the module**, and **not one investigation of any kind is named for uveitis**. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20 says only *\"after doing a systemic work up\"*, and its own audit spells the hole out — *\"NO INVESTIGATION IS NAMED ANYWHERE for uveitis … no chest X-ray, no Mantoux/tuberculin, no serum ACE, no VDRL/FTA-ABS, no anterior chamber tap or PCR\"*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 repeats the same empty phrase, *\"After doing systemic workup\"*, and its NOT-COVERED list confirms *\"No investigation list for uveitis\"*. **The whole option menu is answered below from general medical knowledge, and every clinical claim in this entry is *not taken from the course material* unless a source is cited for it.**\n\n**The principle that makes the answer obvious even without a memorised panel:** uveitis is investigated by testing for **the systemic diseases that cause it**. The module *does* print that list — `L13,14` slide 11 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134/137: **TB, syphilis, leprosy · HSV, HZV, CMV · candidiasis · toxoplasmosis · Behçet, sarcoidosis, multiple sclerosis, ankylosing spondylitis · masquerade malignancies**. So an investigation earns its place if it detects one of those. Read the four options against that list:\n\n| Option | Which taught cause it looks for |\n|---|---|\n| **Chest x-ray** | **Tuberculosis** and **sarcoidosis** — both on the aetiology list, and both are chest diseases with an ocular manifestation. Hilar lymphadenopathy or an apical lesion changes the whole management, because steroids without excluding TB can disseminate it. |\n| **Rheumatologic assessment** | **Ankylosing spondylitis**, psoriatic arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134 groups these as the **HLA-B27** associations) and **juvenile idiopathic arthritis**. Q427 of this topic is an ankylosing-spondylitis vignette; Q436 is a woman with recurrent joint pain and recurrent iritis. Both are asking the student to make the joint–eye connection that this investigation formalises. |\n| **Tuberculin test.** | **Tuberculosis** again, from the other direction — the skin test rather than the film. Together with the chest film it is the classic pair before starting immunosuppression. |\n| **Echocardiography.** | **Nothing on the list.** No taught cause of uveitis is a cardiac disease, and the echocardiogram images valves, chambers and pericardium — none of which shares a mechanism with uveal inflammation. |\n\n**Where the bank's own text supports the reasoning.** The book's treatment N.B. (p.138, repeated on `L13,14` slide 27) states the rule the investigations exist to serve: *\"Although infection is an uncommon cause of uveitis, it is important to **rule it out before instituting immunosuppressive therapy**.\"* That single sentence is why a chest film and a tuberculin test are standard before steroids — and it gives no role to an echocardiogram.\n\n**The one place a heart study does enter ophthalmology**, and it is worth naming so the two are never confused: **echocardiography is an investigation for an EMBOLIC retinal artery occlusion**, looking for a cardiac source of emboli — a **retinal vascular** problem, not a uveal one. Filing it under uveitis is the mistake this question is built to catch. *(Not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**Practical bottom line:** in recurrent or bilateral or granulomatous uveitis, investigate along two axes — **infection** (chest imaging, tuberculin/IGRA, syphilis serology) and **autoimmunity** (rheumatological assessment, HLA-B27 where the picture fits) — plus anything the history points at. A test that maps onto neither axis does not belong. *(The specific panel is **not taken from the course material**; the two axes are the module's own aetiology headings.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Uveitis is investigated by hunting the systemic diseases that cause it — chest film and tuberculin for TB and sarcoid, rheumatological assessment for the HLA-B27 arthropathies — while echocardiography maps onto no taught cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q415), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-416', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The role of **surgery in uveitis** includes all of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Peripheral iridotomy in the presence of pupillary block.','Argon laser photocoagulation in the presence of exudative retinal detachment.','Cataract extraction in the presence of complicated cataract.','External fistulizing surgery in the presence of chronic glaucoma.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Argon laser photocoagulation is used for rhegmatogenous RD (retinal tears), **not exudative RD.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box turns on the mechanism of the detachment, and the module teaches all three mechanisms.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` separates them by what puts the fluid under the retina:\n\n| Type | What lifts the retina | What laser can do about it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rhegmatogenous** | Fluid passes through a **break** and collects in the subretinal space | Laser **seals around the break** — a chorioretinal scar walls the hole off, and the fluid pathway closes. This is the one laser treats. |\n| **Tractional** | Contracting fibrous tissue on the retinal surface pulls it up | Laser does not release traction; vitrectomy does. |\n| **Exudative** | Fluid **leaks out of the choroid** into the subretinal space through an intact retina | ⚠️ **There is no break to seal.** Burning the retina does not stop the choroid leaking, and the treatment is directed at the cause — steroids and immunosuppression when the cause is uveitis. |\n\nExudative detachment is precisely the kind uveitis produces: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 25 lists **retinal detachment** among the complications of posterior uveitis, and the book repeats it (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.137, from Tutorial). So option B pairs a real complication with the one treatment that cannot address it — an argon burn aimed at a leaking choroid.\n\n**Why the other three ARE genuine surgical roles, each answering a complication the module lists:**\n\n| Option | The complication it answers |\n|---|---|\n| **Peripheral iridotomy for pupillary block** | 360° posterior synechiae seal the pupil, aqueous cannot cross from posterior to anterior chamber, the peripheral iris bows forward (**iris bombé**) and the angle closes. An iridotomy makes a second route through the iris and the bombé flattens. ⚠️ **This topic keys against itself here — Q432 describes exactly that eye and keys \"stopping cycloplegics\" instead of iridotomy.** By keying B, *this* question is asserting that option A is TRUE. The two entries name each other; neither key is disputed. |\n| **Cataract extraction for complicated cataract** | `L13,14` slide 19 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 both list **complicated cataract** as a complication of anterior uveitis; once the lens is opaque, only surgery restores the view — of the fundus as well as for the patient. |\n| **External fistulizing surgery for chronic glaucoma** | **Secondary glaucoma** is on every complication list in this topic (slides 18, 22 and the book p.135). When drops fail, a fistulising operation — trabeculectomy — creates a new outflow path. |\n\n**The pattern to carry:** surgery in uveitis is **not** treatment of the inflammation. It is treatment of the **mechanical consequences** — a blocked pupil, an opaque lens, an unrelievable pressure. The inflammation itself is treated with drugs (Q414, Q442).",
    objective:'Surgery in uveitis addresses its mechanical complications — iridotomy for pupil block, lens extraction for complicated cataract, fistulising surgery for secondary glaucoma; argon laser treats rhegmatogenous, not exudative, detachment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q416), key p.69 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-417', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'**Rupture of the choroid** affects the vision markedly:',
    options:['In most cases.','If it is underlying the fovea.','If is caused by blunt trauma.','If covered with hemorrhage.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Choroidal rupture is a break in Bruch’s membrane, commonly due to blunt trauma.\n\nThe severity of visual loss **depends mainly on whether the rupture involves the fovea.**\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n⚠️ **The box is effectively the only teaching the module has on this lesion.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 39 is headed *\"Blunt trauma: Choroidal rupture\"* and carries **a fundus photograph and nothing else** — a concentric crescent temporal to the disc, with no prose beneath it. There is no cached book chapter on trauma. Everything below the box is therefore **not taken from the course material** except where a source is named.\n\n**What the lesion is.** A blunt blow compresses the globe along its axis and stretches it at the equator. The sclera is tough and the retina is elastic, but **Bruch's membrane is neither** — it is a thin, brittle lamina — so it splits, taking the choriocapillaris and the overlying RPE with it. The break heals as a **crescentic white scar concentric with the optic disc**, which is the shape the deck's photograph shows: the tear follows the line of greatest stretch around the nerve head.\n\nThe book supplies the anatomy the box assumes. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.133: *\"**Bruch's membrane** which is the **innermost layer** of the choroid. The basement membrane of the retinal pigment epithelium is a part of Bruch's membrane\"* — the same fact this topic keys at Q434.\n\n**Why the fovea decides everything.** A choroidal rupture is a small, permanent, focal scar. Vision depends on **where** it sits:\n\n| Location of the rupture | Visual outcome |\n|---|---|\n| **Extrafoveal** (the usual case — temporal to the disc, sparing the centre) | Often minimal or no symptoms; a scar found on examination |\n| **Subfoveal / involving the fovea** | **Permanent central visual loss.** The photoreceptors over the break lose their choroidal blood supply, and the fovea has no reserve — there is no other part of the retina that can resolve fine detail |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **In most cases.** | Reverses the usual outcome. Most ruptures are eccentric and spare the fovea, so most eyes see well. |\n| **If is caused by blunt trauma.** | ⚠️ **A misprint — the word \"it\" is missing; the bank prints \"If is caused by blunt trauma.\"** Beyond the typo, the option is circular: blunt trauma is the ordinary cause (the box says *\"commonly due to blunt trauma\"*, and the deck files it under *Blunt trauma*), so it cannot discriminate between eyes that see and eyes that do not. |\n| **If covered with hemorrhage.** | The near-miss with a grain of truth in it. Subretinal haemorrhage over a fresh rupture **does** obscure vision — but it **clears**, and what remains is the scar. Blood is a temporary factor; the location of the break is the permanent one, and the box says the severity depends \"mainly\" on the fovea. |\n\n**The late complication worth knowing** (**not taken from the course material**): a break in Bruch's membrane is a route for **choroidal neovascularisation** to grow through, sometimes months or years later, so an eye with an eccentric rupture and good vision still deserves follow-up and Amsler self-monitoring. The module names choroidal neovascularisation as a complication of posterior uveitis (`L13,14` slide 25) but never connects it to trauma.\n\n*(Filed under Trauma — the lesion is a blunt-injury lesion and its only module source is `L25) Trauma I`. Secondary chapter: Uvea, where the bank prints it and where Bruch's membrane is taught.)*",
    objective:'Choroidal rupture is a break in Bruch’s membrane from blunt trauma; vision is lost permanently only when the break involves the fovea, since overlying haemorrhage clears and the mechanism does not vary.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q417), key p.69 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-418', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'Treatment of **acute iridocyclitis** should **NOT** include:',
    options:['Cortisone drops & ointment','Atropine 1% eye drops','Treatment of the cause','Pilocarpine 2% eye drops','Systemic steroids'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A miotic is the one drug class that is actively harmful in acute anterior uveitis, and the reason is the reverse of everything the treatment aims at.**\n\nBoth sources prescribe the opposite drug. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20: *\"**Cycloplegic-mydriatic** topical medications: ➤ to decrease photophobia and pain from iritis and ciliary spasm ➤ **To prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae**\"*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 prints the same two rationales.\n\n**What pilocarpine would do to this eye — three harms at once:**\n\n| Action of a miotic | Consequence in an inflamed eye |\n|---|---|\n| **Constricts the pupil** | Holds the pupil margin **pressed against the anterior lens capsule**, in maximum contact — the exact position in which fibrinous exudate glues them together. It manufactures the **posterior synechiae** that atropine exists to prevent (Q420, Q438). |\n| **Contracts the ciliary muscle** | The eye already hurts from **ciliary spasm** — that is what the brow ache and photophobia are (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134: *\"Ocular pain with globe tenderness and brow ache (ciliary muscle spasm)\"*). Forcing the spastic muscle to contract harder increases the pain. |\n| **Increases vascular permeability of the iris** | Worsens the protein and cell leak that produces flare and cells. *(Not taken from the course material; the first two harms follow directly from what the sources prescribe.)* |\n\n**Why the other four are all correct:** A and E are the box at Q414 — *\"Steroids (topical- periocular- systemic)\"*, and the bank asks which route is best at Q442. C is item 4 of the same list, *\"Control of the causative condition\"*, which is what the systemic work-up at Q415 is for. B is item 2, the cycloplegic-mydriatic.\n\n⚠️ **Note the drug strengths.** *Atropine 1 %* and *pilocarpine 2 %* are printed by the bank and by nothing else: the book records that **no dose, drop frequency or concentration appears anywhere in its twelve pages**, and the deck names drug classes only. The numbers are the bank's.\n\n**The trap this question sets, and it catches people who have learned glaucoma first.** Pilocarpine is a **treatment** in acute angle-closure glaucoma — `L12) Glaucoma 2` lists *\"Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\"* in its medical management — because there the problem is a pupil-block angle and constricting the pupil pulls the peripheral iris out of the angle. In uveitis the same drug is forbidden. **Two red, painful eyes with reduced vision; opposite drugs.** The sign that separates them is the pupil (Q419), and this topic keys the miotic question three times over: here, at Q435 (topical drugs in uveitic glaucoma) and at Q441 (managing a raised pressure during the attack).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Miotics are contraindicated in acute iridocyclitis — they hold the pupil against the lens where synechiae form and worsen ciliary spasm — while steroids, cycloplegics and treating the cause are the standard four.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q418), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-419', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The most important **differentiating manifestation** between **acute iridocyclitis** and **acute angle closure glaucoma** is:',
    options:['Circum-corneal redness','Diminution of visual acuity','Size of the pupil','Age of onset'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Acute iridocyclitis:\nSmall, constricted (miotic) pupil\nPoorly reacting due to sphincter spasm and posterior synechiae\n\nAcute angle-closure glaucoma:\nMid-dilated, vertically oval pupil\nFixed, non-reactive to light\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box is both syllabus sources side by side.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists among the signs of acute angle closure: *\"**Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil**\"* and *\"Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe (forward bowing)\"*. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 17 gives the other side: *\"Miosis with sluggish pupillary light reaction. Irregular shape due to synechiae\"*. `L37) Red Eye` states both in one paragraph: *\"The pupil in an eye with iridocyclitis typically is somewhat **smaller** than that of the other eye … In acute glaucoma, the pupil is usually **fixed, mid-dilated (5–6 mm)**, and slightly irregular.\"*\n\n**Why the pupil and not the other three — the test is which sign DIFFERS between the two diseases:**\n\n| Option | Iridocyclitis | Acute angle closure | Discriminates? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Circum-corneal redness** | Present — *\"Perilimbal (ciliary) injection with violaceous hue\"* (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134) | Present — *\"Ciliary injection\"* (`L12`) | **No.** Both are ciliary-flush red eyes; that is exactly why they are confused. |\n| **Diminution of visual acuity** | Present — *\"Decreased vision\"* is the first listed sign | Present — *\"Diminution of vision → rapid\"* (`L12`) | **No.** Both drop vision; the tempo differs, the presence does not. |\n| **Size of the pupil** | **Small**, irregular, sluggish | **Mid-dilated**, vertically oval, fixed | **Yes — and in opposite directions.** One is constricted, the other dilated. Nothing else on the list inverts like this. |\n| **Age of onset** | Any age; the module's own vignettes are 27, 32, 36 and 40 (Q427, Q430, Q431, Q432) | Typically older, hyperopic eyes with shallow chambers | **No.** A tendency, not a sign — and it fails on the individual patient, which is where the decision has to be made. |\n\n**Why the pupils go opposite ways.** In iridocyclitis the inflamed iris sphincter goes into **reflex spasm**, and posterior synechiae tether the iris where it touches the lens: small and misshapen. In an acute attack of angle closure the iris is **ischaemic** from the pressure and its sphincter is paralysed at the mid-dilated position that started the block in the first place — the mid-dilated pupil is both the cause (maximum iris–lens contact producing relative pupil block, per `L12`'s flow chart) and the sign.\n\n**Why getting this right matters more than most single signs:** the two diseases take **opposite drugs**. Uveitis gets a **cycloplegic** to dilate; angle closure gets a **miotic** to constrict (`L12`: *\"Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\"*). Q418 and Q441 of this topic are built on that inversion. Two other signs settle any residual doubt at the slit lamp — **anterior chamber depth** (shallow in angle closure, normal in uveitis: `L37) Red Eye`, *\"a shallow anterior chamber should always suggest the possibility of acute angle-closure glaucoma\"*) and **the pressure itself** (stony hard in angle closure; usually **low** in acute iridocyclitis, Q447).",
    objective:'The pupil separates acute iridocyclitis from acute angle closure — small, irregular and sluggish against mid-dilated, oval and fixed — where redness, reduced vision and age occur in both.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.64 (Q419), key p.69 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-420', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'Reasons to use **atropine** in acute iridocyclitis do **NOT** include that it:',
    options:['Dilates the pupil and thus breaks any recent synechiae','Paralyses the ciliary muscle and thus gives rest to the ciliary body','Prevents the formation of synechiae','Keeps the IOP at normal by opening the canal of Schlemm'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Atropine **can actually increase IOP in susceptible patients, especially narrow-angle eyes.** It does not open the canal of Schlemm or directly reduce IOP.\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Options A, B and C are the syllabus rationale for a cycloplegic, and both sources print them.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20: *\"**Cycloplegic-mydriatic** topical medications: ➤ to decrease photophobia and pain from iritis and **ciliary spasm** ➤ **To prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae**\"*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 prints the same, with the synechiae half marked **Tutorial**.\n\n**Atropine is one drug doing two pharmacological jobs, and the three true options split neatly between them:**\n\n| Action | Muscle blocked | Clinical effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mydriasis** | Iris **sphincter** | The pupil dilates, so the iris pulls **away** from the lens. Adhesions that have just formed are torn apart (option A) and new ones cannot form on a moving iris (option C). |\n| **Cycloplegia** | **Ciliary** muscle | The spastic ciliary muscle is paralysed, which is what relieves the brow ache and the photophobia — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134 attributes both to spasm: *\"Photophobia … due to reactive spasm of the muscles of the inflamed iris\"* and *\"Ocular pain with globe tenderness and brow ache (ciliary muscle spasm)\"* (option B). |\n\n**Why option D is false twice over.** First, the anatomy: the **canal of Schlemm is not opened by a drug at all** — it is a circumferential channel outside the trabecular meshwork, and no muscarinic antagonist acts on it. If anything, a *miotic* pulls on the scleral spur and improves trabecular outflow; atropine does the opposite. Second, the direction: as the box says, atropine **raises** pressure in a susceptible eye, because a widely dilated pupil crowds the peripheral iris into a narrow angle. That is why atropine is contraindicated in an eye at risk of angle closure — a caution the module states from the other side, in `L12) Glaucoma 2`, where mid-dilation is the trigger of the whole pupil-block cascade. *(The Schlemm's-canal anatomy and the outflow effect of miotics are **not taken from the course material**; the pressure warning is the bank's own printed box.)*\n\n**The clinical shape of the answer:** three of the four options describe things atropine does **to the iris and ciliary body**, which is where uveitis lives. The false one describes something in the **drainage angle**, which is a different structure with different drugs — and when the pressure does rise in uveitis, the module reaches for beta-blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors instead (Q435, Q441), never for a cycloplegic as a pressure-lowering agent.",
    objective:'Atropine in iridocyclitis dilates to break and prevent posterior synechiae and cycloplegias to rest the spastic ciliary muscle; it does not act on the canal of Schlemm and can raise IOP in a narrow-angle eye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.65 (Q420), key p.69 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-421', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**Pars planitis** is:',
    options:['Anterior uveitis.','Intermediate uveitis.','Posterior uveitis.','Panuveitis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Intermediate uveitis – involves pars plana.**\n\n**Anterior uveitis** – involves iris/ciliary body anteriorly, not pars plana.\n\n**Posterior uveitis** – involves retina/choroid.\n\n**Panuveitis** – inflammation of all uveal segments.\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The name is the answer: *pars planitis* = inflammation of the pars plana, and the pars plana is the territory of intermediate uveitis.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.136 titles the section **\"Intermediate Uveitis (Pars planitis - Cyclitis)\"** and defines it as *\"Intraocular inflammation mainly focused on the vitreous and peripheral retina … It incorporates **pars planitis**, posterior cyclitis, and vitritis.\"* `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 12 gives the same anatomical classification: *\"2. **Intermediate uveitis**: Inflammation predominantly involving the **pars plana**, vitreous, and the peripheral retina.\"*\n\n**The four-part classification the box lays out, with the module's own wording:**\n\n| Type | What is inflamed (`L13,14` slide 12 / book p.133) | The signs that give it away |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Anterior** (iritis / iridocyclitis) | Iris ± ciliary body | Ciliary injection, KPs, cells and flare, miotic irregular pupil |\n| **Intermediate** (pars planitis) | **Pars plana**, vitreous, peripheral retina | **Snowballs** and **snow-banking**, floaters, vitreous cells |\n| **Posterior** (choroiditis / chorioretinitis) | Choroid ± retina | Painless vision loss, floaters, vitreous haze, choroidal lesions |\n| **Panuveitis** | *\"Simultaneous inflammation of all three parts of the uveal tract\"* | The summation of all of the above |\n\n**⚠️ THIS BOX IS THE EVIDENCE AGAINST THIS TOPIC'S OWN Q433.** Twelve questions later the bank asks where snowballs and snow-banking occur and keys **posterior uveitis** — while this box, printed on the same answer page, states that posterior uveitis *\"involves retina/choroid\"* and that the pars plana belongs to intermediate uveitis. Both sources put snowballs and snow-banking under intermediate uveitis by name (`L13,14` slide 21; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.136). **Both keys are recorded exactly as printed and neither is disputed**; the two entries name each other so the contradiction can be seen rather than absorbed.\n\n**What else the module attaches to intermediate uveitis, none of which is in the box:** it *\"Usually affects **younger** patients, aged **15 to 40 years**\"*, is *\"Associated with anterior uveitis in about **50 %** of cases\"*, and causes *\"Macular oedema in **30 %** of cases\"* (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.136). Its complications are cataract, glaucoma and macular oedema; its treatment adds **immunosuppressives** and, uniquely, **surgical options — vitrectomy, cryotherapy, laser photocoagulation** (slide 23).",
    objective:'Pars planitis is intermediate uveitis — inflammation of the pars plana, vitreous and peripheral retina, marked by snowballs and snow-banking — as against anterior (iris/ciliary body), posterior (retina/choroid) and pan-uveitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.65 (Q421), key p.69 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-422', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'Regarding **iris anatomy**:',
    options:['The collarette divides the anterior surface into a wider inner zone and a narrower outer zone.','The sphincter & dilator muscles are derived from mesoderm.','The two pigmented layers on the posterior surface are of neuro-ectodermal origin.','The circulus arteriosus major is located at the collarette'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THREE OF THE FOUR OPTIONS TURN ON FACTS THE MODULE NEVER PRINTS, INCLUDING THE KEYED ONE, AND THE ABSENCES ARE MEASURED.** Grepped case-insensitively across all 29 lecture caches in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and all 12 cached book chapters in `content\\ophtho\\book\\`: **\"neuroectoderm\" and \"neuro-ectoderm\" return 0 hits anywhere in the module**; **\"circulus arterios\" returns 0 hits**, and \"major arterial circle\" occurs exactly once — inside the `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` NOT-COVERED list, stating that the vessel is *never named*. \"mesoderm\" occurs only in `L12) Glaucoma 2`, about the goniodysgenesis membrane, never about iris muscles. Everything below is therefore **not taken from the course material** except where a source is named.\n\n**Option C is true — and it is one of the more useful facts in ocular embryology.** The iris is built from two germ layers at once:\n\n| Part of the iris | Origin | What the module does say |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Stroma** (the front, with its crypts and furrows) | Neural crest / mesenchyme | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.131: *\"**The iris stroma**: It is the front pigmented layer. It has **no epithelium**. The stroma contains the muscles of the iris (**sphincter pupillae and dilator pupillae**)\"* |\n| **The two posterior pigmented layers** | **Neuroectoderm** — they are the forward continuation of the two layers of the optic cup, the same sheet that becomes retina behind and ciliary epithelium in between | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.131 names them: *\"**The iris pigment epithelial cells** posterior to the stroma\"*, but gives no embryology |\n| **Sphincter and dilator pupillae** | **Also neuroectoderm** — uniquely, these are smooth muscles that differentiate out of the anterior epithelial layer, not from mesoderm | — |\n\n**Which is why option B is false.** The iris muscles are the textbook exception to the rule that smooth muscle is mesodermal: they arise from the **outgrowth of the neuroectodermal anterior pigment epithelium**. B states the opposite.\n\n**Option A is inverted, and the book is enough to catch it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.131 divides the iris surface: *\"1. **The pupillary zone** is the **inner** region whose inner edge forms the boundary of the pupil. 2. **The ciliary zone** is the **rest of the iris** that extends to the ciliary body. 3. **The collarette** is the **thickest region** of the iris, separating the pupillary portion from the ciliary portion.\"* The collarette sits roughly at the junction of the inner third and outer two-thirds, so the **outer (ciliary) zone is the WIDER one** — the reverse of what A claims. `L13,14` slide 4 labels the same three landmarks on its photographs: *\"A: Pupillary zone · B: Ciliary zone · C: Collarette · D: Contraction furrows\"*.\n\n**Option D swaps the two arterial circles.** There are two, and they are named for where they lie: the **major arterial circle** sits in the **ciliary body at the iris root**, formed by the long posterior ciliary arteries and the anterior ciliary arteries; the **minor arterial circle** lies at the **collarette**, which is precisely why the collarette is the thickest part — it is where the vessels converge. D puts the major circle at the collarette, which is the minor circle's address. *(Both circles are on the deck's own list of things it never names.)*\n\n**A memory hook for the exam:** *the iris is a neuroectodermal back with a neural-crest front, and its muscles belong to the back.* And *the collarette is the minor circle's ring; the major circle hides in the root.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The posterior pigmented layers of the iris — and its sphincter and dilator — are neuroectodermal; the collarette marks the narrower inner pupillary zone and the minor arterial circle, not the major one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.65 (Q422), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-423', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**Complications of anterior uveitis** include the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Corneal vascularization','Glaucoma','PAS','Occlusion-pupillae'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The complication lists in both sources are short and neither contains corneal vascularization.**\n\n| Source | Its complication list, verbatim |\n|---|---|\n| `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slides 18–19 | *Posterior synechiae · Secondary glaucoma · Complicated Cataract · Band Shaped Keratopathy* |\n| `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 | *2ry glaucoma · Posterior synechiae · Complicated cataract · **Calcific band keratopathy** (if the iritis becomes chronic) especially in juvenile idiopathic arthritis · Macular edema (if the iritis becomes chronic)* |\n\n**Why the three true options are true, and each is one step down the same chain:**\n\n| Option | What it is, and how uveitis produces it |\n|---|---|\n| **Glaucoma** | On both lists by name. Inflammatory debris blocks the meshwork, synechiae close the angle or block the pupil, or steroids raise the pressure — the three mechanisms Q440 and Q447 set out. |\n| **PAS** | ⚠️ **The bank never expands the abbreviation here** — it prints it as \"P.A.S.\" one page later, at Q440: *\"Organization of inflammatory exudates results in formation of **P.A.S.**\"* **Peripheral anterior synechiae**: the peripheral iris adheres to the trabecular meshwork and cornea, permanently sealing the angle. That is one of the routes to the glaucoma in option B. |\n| **Occlusion-pupillae** | ⚠️ **A term the module never prints** — the deck's own audit lists *\"SECLUSIO PUPILLAE, IRIS BOMBÉ and PUPILLARY BLOCK are not printed anywhere in the deck\"*, and the book chapter is no better. The pair, since the bank expects them: ***seclusio* pupillae** = the pupil margin adherent to the lens for the full 360°, so aqueous cannot pass forward → pupil block → **iris bombé** (Q424, Q432). ***Occlusio* pupillae** = the pupil aperture itself filled by an organised inflammatory membrane, so it is occluded rather than merely bound. Both are end-stage consequences of posterior synechiae, which is the very first item on the deck's list. *(These definitions are **not taken from the course material**.)* |\n\n**Why corneal vascularization is the odd one out.** New vessels growing into the corneal stroma are a response to something happening **in the cornea** — chronic hypoxia (contact lens overwear), chronic surface inflammation, interstitial keratitis, a healed ulcer, chemical burn or limbal stem-cell failure. Anterior uveitis inflames the iris and ciliary body **behind** the cornea; the corneal insult it does produce is on the endothelium and Bowman's layer — **keratic precipitates** (deposits, not vessels) and **band keratopathy** (calcium in Bowman's layer, which is avascular). Neither involves vessels. *(The causes of corneal vascularization are **not taken from the course material**; the KP and band-keratopathy associations are both printed by both sources.)*\n\n**The distinction to hold on to:** uveitis damages the eye by **sticking things together** (synechiae, PAS, seclusio, occlusio), by **clouding media** (cataract, band keratopathy) and by **raising or dropping pressure**. Growing new vessels into clear tissue is a different disease process — one that belongs to the cornea and, on the iris, to ischaemia rather than inflammation (rubeosis, `L13,14` slide 9).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Anterior uveitis complicates as synechiae (including PAS and occlusio pupillae), secondary glaucoma, complicated cataract and band keratopathy; corneal vascularization is a corneal disease, not a uveitic one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.65 (Q423), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-424', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The photograph is an external close-up of one eye in diffuse illumination, lids and lashes in frame. **The cornea is hazy. The iris is blue-grey, its surface smooth and free of any fine branching red vessels, and the peripheral iris stands noticeably closer to the cornea than the centre does. The pupil is small and distorted, with whitish material and radial strands at its margin.** Which of the following best describes the photo?',
    options:['Iris bombe-posterior synechiae','Robeosis iridis-posterior synechiae','Iris bombe-neovascular formation','Robeosis irides-neovascular formation'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE, and the bank's printed stem is only \"Which of the following best describes the photo?\" — it names nothing, so it cannot be answered without the image. The bold restatement above lists **what is visible** and stops there; converting those observations into the two named signs is the question. **No crop was cut**: the photograph's page, column and 400 dpi bounds are recorded in `gg-t10.array.js`. ⚠️ **The bank misprints \"Robeosis\" for \"Rubeosis\" in both B and D, and cannot decide on the ending — B prints \"iridis\", D prints \"irides\".** Options are transcribed exactly as printed.)*\n\n**Each option is a pair of signs, and the photograph has to satisfy both halves. The two variables are: is the iris bowed forward or flat, and are there new vessels on it or not?**\n\n**Half one — iris bombé, not a flat iris.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` gives the sign in its acute angle-closure list as *\"Shallow anterior chamber / **Iris bombe (forward bowing)**\"*, and its pathogenesis flow chart shows how it arises: *relative pupil block → accumulation of aqueous humour behind the iris → **iris bombe** → narrowing of the angle of the anterior chamber*. In this eye the periphery of the iris is closer to the cornea than the centre — the chamber is shallow peripherally and deeper centrally — which is the convex profile the term names.\n\n**Half two — posterior synechiae, not neovascular formation.** Rubeosis iridis is a **vascular** sign: fine, irregular, branching red vessels creeping over the iris surface and round the pupil margin, which `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 9 photographs (*\"a close-up of an injected eye with fine new vessels running over the iris to the pupil margin\"*). **This iris carries none.** What it carries instead is a distorted pupil with whitish material and strands at its margin — the appearance of an iris tethered to the lens by inflammatory adhesion. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 pictures the same thing: *\"Posterior synechiae ×2 (irregular festooned pupil after dilatation)\"*.\n\n**Why the two signs belong together in one eye — this is the point of the question.** Posterior synechiae that run the full 360° seal the pupil margin to the lens (*seclusio pupillae*). Aqueous secreted by the ciliary body then has no route from the posterior chamber to the anterior chamber, pressure builds **behind** the iris, and the iris balloons forward — **iris bombé**. The chain is *uveitis → posterior synechiae → pupil block → iris bombé → angle closure → secondary glaucoma*, and the module lists both ends of it: posterior synechiae and secondary glaucoma sit next to each other on `L13,14` slide 18. Q432 of this topic is the same eye presented as a vignette; Q416 keys **peripheral iridotomy** as its treatment.\n\n**Why the other three fail:**\n\n| Option | Which half fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Robeosis iridis-posterior synechiae** | Gets the synechiae right, invents the vessels. Rubeosis is a sign of **ischaemia** — diabetic retinopathy, ischaemic vein occlusion — not of adhesion. |\n| **Iris bombe-neovascular formation** | Gets the bombé right, then names the wrong cause of it. Neovascular membrane closes an angle by **pulling** the peripheral iris across the trabeculum (`L12`: *\"Neovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum\"*) — a flat iris dragged forward at the root, not a ballooned one. |\n| **Robeosis irides-neovascular formation** | Both halves wrong, and the pair is internally redundant — rubeosis *is* neovascular formation on the iris, so the option says one thing twice. |\n\n**Bottom line at the slit lamp:** a convex iris with a bound-down pupil is inflammatory pupil block; a flat iris with red vessels crawling over it is ischaemic rubeosis. Two different diseases, two different treatments — iridotomy for the first, retinal ablation and anti-VEGF for the second.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A convex, forward-bowed iris with a distorted pupil bound by adhesions and no new vessels is iris bombé from posterior synechiae — the pupil-block chain of chronic anterior uveitis, not rubeosis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.65 (Q424, figure), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-425', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea-surg',
    stem:'The photograph is an external close-up of one eye of an older, pale-skinned patient — wrinkled lid skin, sparse lashes. **The iris is brown, the pupil round and central, the sclera white and quiet. The one abnormality is a complete grey-white ring in the periphery of the cornea, separated from the limbus by a narrow clear interval and densest above and below.** What is the treatment for the following photo?',
    options:['Surgery','Steroid','No treatment','Laser'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE and the bank's printed stem — \"What is the treatment for the following photo?\" — names no condition at all, so it cannot be answered without the image. The bold restatement above describes only what is visible. **No crop was cut**; the page, column and 400 dpi bounds are in `gg-t10.array.js`.)*\n\n**The ring is arcus senilis, and the module teaches it in one line.** `L15,16) Cornea`, under **Corneal Degenerations**: *\"**Age-related: Arcus senilis (lipid arc at limbus – physiological in elderly, check lipids if <45 yrs)**\"*. That single line contains both halves of the answer — *physiological* and the age caveat.\n\n**Why \"no treatment\" is right, and not merely a default:**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **What it is** | Lipid deposited in the corneal periphery, in the deep and superficial stroma. |\n| **Where it sits** | The **periphery only**, with a **clear interval** separating it from the limbus — the feature visible in this photograph and the one that identifies it. |\n| **What it does to vision** | **Nothing.** It never reaches the visual axis; the pupillary zone of the cornea stays clear. There is no symptom to treat. |\n| **What it means** | In an elderly patient, ageing and nothing more — the deck's word is *physiological*. |\n| **⚠️ The one exception** | **Under 45, check the lipids** (`L15,16`). Arcus in a young person may be the visible sign of a familial hyperlipidaemia — so the action is a **blood test**, still not an eye treatment. |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, one by one:**\n\n| Option | What it treats, and why not here |\n|---|---|\n| **Surgery** | Reserved for opacities that block vision or threaten the eye — a corneal graft for a scarred axis, cataract extraction for an opaque lens. An asymptomatic peripheral lipid ring threatens neither, and no operation removes it. |\n| **Steroid** | Treats **inflammation**. This eye is white and quiet: no injection, no discharge, no cells. Arcus is a **degeneration** — deposition, not inflammation — so steroids have nothing to act on, and their own complications (glaucoma, cataract) would be the only effect. |\n| **Laser** | Treats retinal and angle disease and, in refractive surgery, reshapes the central cornea. Neither applies. |\n\n**⚠️ Two things to note about this question's placement.** First, **it is not a uveal condition at all** — the bank prints it inside the Uveal Tract topic, and it is filed here under Cornea, where the module teaches corneal degenerations. Second, **do not confuse the ring with band keratopathy**, which *is* a uveitis complication and appears twice in this topic: band keratopathy is **calcium in Bowman's layer** lying in the **interpalpebral strip** and crossing the cornea horizontally through the axis (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.135, *\"Calcific band keratopathy (if the iritis becomes chronic)\"*; `L15,16`, *\"calcium deposits in Bowman's layer in interpalpebral zone\"*). It **does** affect vision, and it **does** have a treatment — *\"EDTA chelation\"*. A peripheral ring with a clear interval is arcus and needs nothing; a horizontal band crossing the pupil is band keratopathy and needs chelation.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A complete grey-white peripheral corneal ring with a clear interval in an elderly eye is arcus senilis — physiological and untreated; under 45 it prompts a lipid profile, not an eye treatment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.65 (Q425, figure), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-426', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The photograph is a dark-field slit-lamp view of one anterior segment, seen as a circular field on black. **A broad grey-white opacity with a bright specular highlight occupies the upper-left of the field; brown iris tissue fills the lower and right portions and appears drawn up into the opacity, with a notch where the two meet.** What is the name of this sign?',
    options:['Anterior synechiae','Posterior synechiae','Iris bombe','Aniridia'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE and the bank's printed stem is only \"What is the name of this sign?\" — unanswerable without it. The bold restatement above describes what is visible. **No crop was cut**; page, column and 400 dpi bounds are in `gg-t10.array.js`. ⚠️ **Honest caveat: this photograph is poorly exposed and genuinely hard to read at any magnification.** The restatement records what can be made out, not a confident reading of a clear image.)*\n\n**Iris drawn forward and adherent to the back of the cornea is an anterior synechia.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 7 names both kinds and pictures both — *\"Iris Synechiae • Posterior synechiae • Anterior synechiae\"* — with *\"a slit-lamp optical-section view in the dark, the slit beam crossing cornea, an anterior chamber and the iris, demonstrating an anterior synechia at the angle\"*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.141 does the same, captioning its figure *\"**Anterior synechiae** (slit-beam photograph, iris adherent to the cornea)\"*.\n\n⚠️ **Neither source ever defines either term in words.** The book's own audit of that page says the six named iris conditions there are *\"LISTED and PICTURED but NOT DEFINED anywhere in the chapter\"*, and the deck's audit says the same. The definitions below are therefore **not taken from the course material**; the two names and their pictures are.\n\n**The pair, and the whole distinction is which structure the iris is stuck to:**\n\n| | **Anterior synechiae** | **Posterior synechiae** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Iris adheres to | **Cornea / trabecular meshwork** (in front) | **Anterior lens capsule** (behind) |\n| Where you look | The **angle** and peripheral cornea, on gonioscopy or an optical section | The **pupil margin**, best after dilatation |\n| What it looks like | Peaked or tented iris running forward to the cornea | An irregular, **festooned** pupil that dilates in petals |\n| Consequence | The angle is sealed → **secondary glaucoma** directly (the \"P.A.S.\" of Q423 and Q440) | Pupil sealed → **pupil block → iris bombé** → angle closure (Q424, Q432) |\n| Typical setting | After a flat chamber, a perforated ulcer, chronic angle closure, or organised inflammatory exudate | Any anterior uveitis |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it would look like instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Posterior synechiae** | The adhesion is behind the iris, so what you see is the **pupil's shape**, not a strand running to the cornea — an irregular festooned outline, best seen dilated. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 pictures exactly that. |\n| **Iris bombe** | Not an adhesion at all but a **profile**: the whole peripheral iris bowed convexly forward, shallowing the chamber all the way round (`L12) Glaucoma 2`, *\"Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe (forward bowing)\"*). It is the *result* of 360° posterior synechiae, and it is diffuse, not focal. |\n| **Aniridia** | Absence of the iris. `L13,14` slide 8 photographs it as *\"an eye with essentially no visible iris, the whole lens/red reflex exposed to the limbus\"*. This photograph is full of iris. |\n\n**Bottom line:** name a synechia by what the iris is stuck **to** — forward to the cornea is anterior and closes the angle mechanically; backward to the lens is posterior and closes the pupil. Both end in secondary glaucoma by different routes.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Iris adherent forward to the cornea or angle is an anterior synechia, which seals the angle directly; posterior synechiae bind iris to lens and cause a festooned pupil and pupil block.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.65 (Q426, figure), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-427', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'A **32-year-old male with a history of ankylosing spondylitis** presents with severe **photophobia**, tearing, periorbital pain, and blurred vision. Slit lamp shows **perilimbal injection and aqueous flare**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Posterior uveitis','Acute anterior uveitis','Intermediate uveitis','Allergic conjunctivitis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Every element of this vignette is on the anterior-uveitis list, and one of them — the ankylosing spondylitis — is on nobody else's.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134 gives the aetiology, and the systemic disease is the first thing it names after \"idiopathic\":\n\n> **Aetiology:** 1. **Idiopathic (75 % of cases)** 2. **Connective tissue diseases** e.g., o **HLA-B27 associated: Ankylosing spondylitis**, psoriatic arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease) o Non-HLA-B27: juvenile idiopathic arthritis.\n\n⚠️ **The HLA-B27 link is absent from the lecture deck** — `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 11 lists *\"ankylosing spondylitis\"* among immune-mediated causes but never prints the antigen, and its audit records the gap explicitly (*\"HLA-B27 by name … never printed\"*). **The book is the source here.** *(That HLA-B27 anterior uveitis is characteristically acute, unilateral, recurrent, alternating between eyes and often with a fibrinous or hypopyon reaction is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Matching the findings to the sign list** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134, `L13,14` slides 13–17):\n\n| Finding in the stem | Where the module puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Photophobia** | Symptom 1 of anterior uveitis — *\"due to reactive spasm of the muscles of the inflamed iris\"* |\n| Tearing | *\"Lacrimation\"*, symptom 3 |\n| **Periorbital pain** | *\"Ocular pain with globe tenderness and **brow ache** (ciliary muscle spasm)\"* |\n| Blurred vision | *\"Decreased vision\"* — both a symptom and a sign |\n| **Perilimbal injection** | *\"**Perilimbal (ciliary) injection** with violaceous hue\"* — the first sign after reduced vision |\n| **Aqueous flare** | The sign this topic keys as the **earliest** feature at Q413 |\n\n**Why the other three do not fit:**\n\n| Option | What is missing or wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Posterior uveitis** | The book's defining symptom is *\"**Painless** diminution of vision\"* with **floaters** (p.137). This patient's dominant complaints are **pain and photophobia**, and the abnormality is in the **anterior chamber**. |\n| **Intermediate uveitis** | *\"Presentation is typically **insidious**\"* with **floaters** and blurred vision (p.136); the signs are in the vitreous and pars plana — snowballs, snow-banking. Nothing in this stem is insidious and nothing is posterior. |\n| **Allergic conjunctivitis** | **Itching**, bilateral, with discharge, and — decisively — no anterior chamber reaction. `L37) Red Eye` is blunt about the discriminators: reduced acuity *\"never occurs in simple conjunctivitis unless there is associated corneal involvement\"*, ciliary flush *\"is not present in conjunctivitis\"*, and *\"Conjunctivitis does not affect the pupil.\"* **Aqueous flare alone excludes it** — a conjunctival disease cannot put protein into the anterior chamber. |\n\n**This vignette runs on into the next two questions**: Q428 asks for the sign expected early in the same case (miosis) and Q429 for its first-line treatment (steroids plus cycloplegics). Read as a set, they are the three questions any anterior uveitis has to answer — what is it, what will I see first, what do I give.\n\n**Clinical bottom line:** a young adult with a red, photophobic, aching eye and *any* seronegative spondyloarthropathy in the history has acute anterior uveitis until proved otherwise — and the eye disease is often what brings the systemic diagnosis to light, which is why Q415 keys **rheumatological assessment** as a useful investigation.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ankylosing spondylitis with a photophobic, aching red eye showing perilimbal injection and aqueous flare is acute anterior uveitis — the HLA-B27 association the book names as its leading non-idiopathic cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q427), key p.69' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-428', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'In the previous case — the **32-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis**, severe photophobia, tearing, periorbital pain and blurred vision, whose slit lamp showed **perilimbal injection and aqueous flare** — which sign would you expect **early** in the disease?',
    options:['Hypopyon','Miosis','Kayser–Fleischer ring','Optic disc swelling'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ The bank prints only \"In the previous case, which sign would you expect early in the disease?\". The antecedent is `opqb-t10-427`, the question immediately above it on book p.66, and its findings are restated above so this entry stands alone. The restatement deliberately stops short of naming the diagnosis, because naming it is what Q427 asks for.)*\n\n**The book prints the word \"early\" beside exactly one sign.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134, in the sign list of anterior uveitis: **\"Miosis (an early sign) with sluggish pupillary light reaction.\"** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 17 gives the same sign without the timing: *\"Pupil • Miosis with sluggish pupillary light reaction. • Irregular shape due to synechiae\"*.\n\n**Why miosis is early.** It requires nothing to accumulate. The inflamed iris sphincter goes into **reflex spasm** the moment the iris is irritated — `L37) Red Eye`: *\"The pupil in an eye with iridocyclitis typically is somewhat smaller than that of the other eye, due to **reflex spasm of the iris sphincter muscle**\"* — so a small pupil is present at the first examination, alongside the flare this patient already has (Q413 keys flare as the **earliest feature**).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each is wrong in a different way:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Hypopyon** | Right disease, **wrong stage**. It is a **late and severe** sign — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134 defines it as *\"a collection of **neutrophils and exudates** in the inferior part of the AC\"*, which needs cells to pour in and settle. Its appearance in a young man with a spondyloarthropathy would be characteristic of HLA-B27 disease or Behçet (Q445), but it is not what you meet first. |\n| **Kayser–Fleischer ring** | **Wrong disease entirely.** A ring of copper deposited in Descemet's membrane at the corneal periphery, the ocular sign of **Wilson disease**. It is a corneal deposit, painless, chronic, and has nothing to do with iritis. *(Not taken from the course material — \"Kayser\" appears nowhere in the uvea deck or book chapter.)* |\n| **Optic disc swelling** | **Wrong compartment.** A posterior-segment sign. Where the module does attach disc oedema to uveal disease it is to **sympathetic ophthalmia** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.140: *\"Iris nodules · Mutton fat KPs · **Disc oedema**\"*), a bilateral granulomatous panuveitis after penetrating trauma — a different disease from this one, and the one Q431 is built around. |\n\n**How to answer any \"which sign is early\" question in this topic.** Rank the signs by how much has to happen before they are visible: **flare** (protein leaks — instant) → **miosis** (muscle spasm — instant) → **cells** → **KPs** (cells must clump on the endothelium) → **hypopyon** (cells must accumulate into a layer) → **posterior synechiae** (fibrin must organise) → **band keratopathy** and **complicated cataract** (chronicity). Q413, this question and Q438 are three points on that single line.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Miosis from reflex sphincter spasm is an early sign of anterior uveitis; hypopyon is late and severe, a Kayser–Fleischer ring belongs to Wilson disease and disc swelling to posterior disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q428), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-429', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'In the previous case — the **32-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis**, severe photophobia, tearing, periorbital pain and blurred vision, with **perilimbal injection and aqueous flare** on slit lamp — what is the appropriate **first-line treatment**?',
    options:['Steroids + antibiotics','Steroids + cycloplegics','Steroids + miotics','Steroids + nsaids'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ The bank prints only \"In the previous case, what is the appropriate first-line treatment?\". **The antecedent is `opqb-t10-427`, not the question immediately above it** — `opqb-t10-428` is not a case, it asks for one sign of Q427's case, so the vignette is still the ankylosing-spondylitis patient two questions up. Its findings are restated above and the diagnosis is deliberately left unnamed, since that is what Q427 asks. Option D prints \"nsaids\" in lower case, as transcribed.)*\n\n**Steroids plus a cycloplegic is the first line, and both sources print exactly that pair as items 1 and 2 of the treatment.**\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135, in the same order:\n\n> 1. **Steroids (topical – periocular – systemic)** to control the inflammation and decrease the incidence of complications\n> 2. **Cycloplegic-mydriatic topical medications:** to decrease photophobia and pain from iris and ciliary spasm (+ to prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae)\n\n**Each drug does something the other cannot:**\n\n| Drug | What it fixes |\n|---|---|\n| **Steroid** | The inflammation itself — the leak that produces flare and cells, and with it the risk of every complication on the list (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.138 N.B.: *\"Corticosteroids are the mainstay of therapy in treating noninfectious causes\"*). |\n| **Cycloplegic** | The two things steroids do not touch: the **pain**, which is ciliary and iris **spasm**, and the **synechiae**, which form while the pupil is small and still. Dilating breaks fresh adhesions and prevents new ones. |\n\nThis patient's dominant symptoms — **photophobia and periorbital ache** — are the spasm symptoms, so the cycloplegic is also the drug that will make him comfortable fastest.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Steroids + antibiotics** | There is no infection here. The aetiology is an **HLA-B27 immune-mediated** uveitis (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134). Antibiotics are the reflex answer to a red painful eye and the wrong one — but note the module's own caution the other way round (p.138): *\"Although infection is an uncommon cause of uveitis, it is important to **rule it out before instituting immunosuppressive therapy**\"* — rule out, not treat blindly. |\n| **Steroids + miotics** | ⚠️ **The exactly-wrong answer.** A miotic holds the pupil small and pressed against the lens, manufacturing the posterior synechiae the treatment exists to prevent, and forces an already spastic ciliary muscle to contract harder. This topic keys the same point three times — Q418, Q435 and Q441. |\n| **Steroids + nsaids** | Not wrong so much as **not the pair the module teaches**. Oral NSAIDs are the first line in `L13,14`'s *episcleritis* management (slide 33, *\"Oral NSAIDs such as ibuprofen is the main line of treatment\"*) and appear in its scleritis treatment (slide 39). They appear **nowhere** in the treatment of uveitis, in either source. |\n\n**The one-line rule:** *anterior uveitis gets a steroid and a dilator — never a constrictor.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'First-line treatment of acute anterior uveitis is a topical steroid plus a cycloplegic-mydriatic — the cycloplegic relieving spasm and preventing synechiae — with miotics contraindicated.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q429), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-430', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'A **27-year-old woman** presents with **floaters** and blurred central vision. Fundus exam shows **choroidal lesions with overlying vitritis**. Which cause is most common?',
    options:['Toxoplasmosis','HSV','Candida','CMV in healthy adults'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The book ranks it in one parenthesis.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.137, aetiology of **posterior uveitis**: *\"**Parasitic**: **toxoplasmosis (most common)**, toxocariasis.\"* That is the whole answer, and it is the only place in the module where any cause of uveitis is ranked. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 11 lists toxoplasmosis among parasitic causes but ranks nothing.\n\n**The vignette is a textbook posterior uveitis**, and the book's own definition and symptom list match it item for item (p.137): *\"Inflammation of the **choroid** with or without inflammation of the retina\"*, symptoms *\"**Painless** diminution of vision · **Floaters**\"*, signs *\"Vitreous haziness, cells, and opacities\"*. Choroidal lesions with overlying vitritis is that picture in two phrases.\n\n**Why toxoplasmosis and not the others:**\n\n| Option | Where it belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **HSV** | On the aetiology list — *\"**Viral**: HSV, HZV, CMV\"* (p.137) — but in the anterior segment it is far better known for **keratitis** and for **trabeculitis**, the mechanism the book gives for a raised pressure in iritis (p.135). Posteriorly it causes acute retinal necrosis, which is rare. |\n| **Candida** | Listed under *\"**Fungal**: histoplasmosis, candidiasis\"*, and it is a real cause of a choroidal lesion with vitritis — but in a specific patient: intravenous drug use, indwelling lines, immunosuppression, prolonged intensive care. A healthy 27-year-old is not that patient. |\n| **CMV in healthy adults** | ⚠️ **The option is self-defeating, and the book says why.** It prints CMV as *\"especially with acquired immunedeficiency [sic] syndrome\"*. CMV retinitis is an **opportunistic** infection of the profoundly immunosuppressed; in a healthy adult it is essentially a non-entity. The qualifier \"in healthy adults\" converts a real cause into a wrong answer. |\n\n**What the module does NOT teach about ocular toxoplasmosis**, so that the gap is visible rather than assumed: **no fundus appearance, no clinical description, no investigation and no treatment for it appears anywhere.** The deck's audit says so explicitly — *\"TOXOPLASMOSIS, TUBERCULOSIS, SYPHILIS, HSV/HZV/CMV and SARCOIDOSIS are likewise names on the slide 11 list only. No clinical picture, no fundus appearance and no specific treatment is given for any of them.\"* *(For orientation only, and **not taken from the course material**: the classic lesion is a focal necrotising retinochoroiditis, often a fresh fluffy white focus at the edge of an old pigmented scar — reactivation of congenital disease — with heavy vitritis giving the \"headlight in fog\" appearance. That reactivation pattern is why the commonest cause of posterior uveitis in an otherwise healthy young adult is a congenital infection.)*\n\n**The reasoning to carry into an exam:** posterior uveitis in a **healthy** patient → think **toxoplasma**; posterior uveitis in an **immunosuppressed** patient → think **CMV**, candida, or another opportunist. The host is half the question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Toxoplasmosis is the commonest cause of posterior uveitis in an immunocompetent patient — the book’s only ranked aetiology — while CMV and candida require an immunosuppressed host.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q430), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-431', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'A **40-year-old woman** presents with severe photophobia, floaters, decreased vision, **vitiligo patches on skin**, and **neurological manifestation**. Which finding supports the diagnosis most strongly?',
    options:['Unilateral disease only','History of penetrating ocular trauma','Bilateral granulomatous inflammation','Hypopyon'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The vignette is Vogt–Koyanagi–Harada syndrome, and the book prints its diagnostic criteria as a numbered list of five.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.140:\n\n> **VKH Syndrome** is an **idiopathic multisystem autoimmune** disease featuring inflammation of melanocyte-containing tissues such as the **uvea, skin, ear, and meninges**.\n>\n> **The following criteria are required to make a diagnosis of VKH syndrome:** 1. The absence of a history of penetrating ocular trauma 2. Absence of other ocular disease entities 3. **Bilateral granulomatous panuveitis** 4. Neurological and auditory manifestations 5. Affection of the skin and its adnexa e.g., vitiligo, alopecia and poliosis\n\n⚠️ **VKH is entirely absent from the lecture deck** — `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera`'s own audit lists it under *\"UVEITIS — NAMED ENTITIES THE DECK DOES NOT TEACH: VOGT–KOYANAGI–HARADA (VKH) syndrome. Entirely absent.\"* So is *granulomatous* as a term, and so is *mutton-fat KP*. **All of it is in the book**, which the module treats as its main reference — this entry is a clean example of why the deck's zeros must never be read as the module's.\n\n**Reading the vignette against the criteria:** vitiligo satisfies criterion 5, the neurological manifestation satisfies criterion 4, and the photophobia, floaters and reduced vision are the panuveitis — anterior symptoms and posterior symptoms in the same eye. **What is missing from the stem is criterion 3**, and that is exactly what the question asks for: *\"Bilateral granulomatous panuveitis\"*.\n\n**Why the other three are not merely unhelpful but actively point away:**\n\n| Option | What it does to the diagnosis |\n|---|---|\n| **Unilateral disease only** | **Excludes** it. Criterion 3 requires **bilateral** disease. VKH inflames melanocytes wherever they are, and the eyes are symmetrical organs — a strictly one-eyed disease is a different diagnosis. |\n| **History of penetrating ocular trauma** | ⚠️ **Excludes VKH by criterion 1, and names its twin.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.140, immediately below the VKH block: *\"**Sympathetic Ophthalmia** ⇨ It is a bilateral granulomatous panuveitis occurring **after penetrating trauma to one eye** … due to immune sensitization to melanin or melanin-associated proteins in uveal tissues.\"* The two diseases are pathologically the same — an autoimmune attack on uveal melanocytes — and **trauma is the single question that separates them.** That is why criterion 1 is an *absence*. |\n| **Hypopyon** | Non-specific and points elsewhere. It is an ordinary sign of any severe anterior uveitis (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134) and is classically associated with **Behçet disease** — which Q445 of this topic pairs with oral and genital ulcers. It says nothing about melanocytes, skin or meninges. |\n\n**⚠️ A TYPESETTING FAULT IN THE ANSWER SECTION, RECORDED BECAUSE IT AFFECTS THIS ENTRY.** The bank prints the **VKH explanation box one key lower, under `opqb-t10-432`** — a question about iris bombé that has nothing to do with VKH. Verified at 400 dpi on book p.70: the lines run *\"431. Correct Answer: C / 432. Correct Answer: C / Explanation: Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH) Syndrome Diagnosis: …\"*. Because a box in this bank always follows the key line it belongs to, **the box is captured verbatim on `opqb-t10-432` where it is printed**, and this entry is treated as having none. The content, however, is plainly this question's. Nothing is corrected; both entries name the other.\n\n**What the book does NOT give for VKH**, so no one goes looking: **no treatment, no staging, and no sunset-glow fundus** — its own note records the absence. What it teaches is the five criteria and the melanocyte mechanism, and that is what this question tests.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'VKH is diagnosed on five criteria, of which bilateral granulomatous panuveitis is the ocular core; a history of penetrating trauma excludes it and names sympathetic ophthalmia instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q431), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-432', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'A **36-year-old woman with recurrent anterior uveitis** presents with a **shallow anterior chamber and bowing forward of the peripheral iris**. Which treatment is indicated?',
    options:['Peripheral iridotomy','Pilocarpine','Stopping cycloplegics','No treatment'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH) Syndrome Diagnosis: The following criteria are required to make a diagnosis of VKH syndrome\n\n1. The absence of a history of penetrating ocular trauma\n2. Absence of other ocular disease entities\n3. **Bilateral granulomatous Panuveitis**\n4. Neurological and auditory manifestations\n5. Affection of the skin and its adnexa e.g., vitiligo, alopecia and poliosis\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim from where it is printed. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️⚠️ TWO PRINTED FAULTS SIT ON THIS ONE ENTRY, AND BOTH ARE RECORDED RATHER THAN CORRECTED.**\n\n**Fault 1 — the box above is about a different question.** It is the VKH diagnostic list, word for word from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.140, and VKH is the subject of **`opqb-t10-431`**, the vignette immediately before this one. This question is about iris bombé and mentions no melanocyte disease at all. Verified at 400 dpi on book p.70: the box is printed **under the key line \"432. Correct Answer: C\"**, and in this bank a box always follows the key it belongs to — so it is captured here, where it appears, and `opqb-t10-431` is recorded as having no box. **The two entries name each other.**\n\n**Fault 2 — the key does not fit the stem, and the bank contradicts itself twelve questions earlier.** The stem describes **iris bombé from pupil block**: recurrent anterior uveitis has bound the pupil margin to the lens through 360° (*seclusio pupillae*), aqueous can no longer pass from the posterior to the anterior chamber, pressure builds behind the iris and the peripheral iris balloons forward, shallowing the angle. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` prints the mechanism as a flow chart — *relative pupil block → accumulation of aqueous humour behind the iris → **iris bombe** → narrowing of the angle of the anterior chamber → increased resistance to aqueous humour outflow* — and lists the sign as *\"Shallow anterior chamber / **Iris bombe (forward bowing)**\"*. **The treatment is to make a new hole in the iris: a peripheral iridotomy**, which `L12` gives as the surgical treatment of angle closure (*\"Iridotomy — Laser (standard) — Surgical\"*), and which **`opqb-t10-416` of this very topic keys as a TRUE role of surgery in uveitis** (*\"Peripheral iridotomy in the presence of pupillary block\"* — that question keys B, so option A stands as correct).\n\n**The bank keys C, \"Stopping cycloplegics\", and that is kept exactly as printed. A defective key is noted, never disputed.** The reading that would rescue it does not survive contact with the sources: cycloplegia is the drug that **prevents and breaks** posterior synechiae in the first place (`L13,14` slide 20: *\"To prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae\"*), so stopping it removes the only thing holding the pupil mobile. **If this exact question appears, answer C as this bank prints it; if a question asks how iris bombé is treated, the answer is a peripheral iridotomy.**\n\n**Why the two remaining options are wrong under any reading:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Pilocarpine** | A miotic — the one drug class contraindicated in uveitis (Q418, Q429, Q435, Q441). It would pull the pupil margin harder against the lens, tightening the seclusio that caused the block. In *primary* angle closure a miotic helps because it drags the peripheral iris out of the angle; in **pupil-block bombé from synechiae** the pupil is already stuck, so constricting it can only make things worse. |\n| **No treatment** | The eye is heading for **secondary angle-closure glaucoma** — the complication both sources list first (`L13,14` slide 18, *\"Posterior synechiae · Secondary glaucoma\"*). Watching is not an option. |\n\n**The picture this vignette describes is the one photographed at `opqb-t10-424`** — a convex iris with a bound-down, distorted pupil. Read the two together.",
    objective:'Recurrent anterior uveitis binding the pupil for 360° causes pupil block and iris bombé, whose treatment is a peripheral iridotomy — a point this bank keys at Q416 and keys against here.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q432), key p.70 (explanation box, printed here but describing Q431)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-433', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**Snowballs and snowbanking** occur in:',
    options:['Posterior uveitis','Acute iritis','Intermediate uveitis','Phacolytic glaucoma'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️⚠️ THE PRINTED KEY IS A, \"POSTERIOR UVEITIS\". IT IS RECORDED EXACTLY AS PRINTED AND IT IS NOT DISPUTED — but both sources, and the bank's own explanation box twelve questions earlier, put snowballs and snow-banking in INTERMEDIATE uveitis (option C). The discrepancy is set out here so the question can still be learned from.** The key line was re-read on a 400 dpi render of book p.70 and is unambiguously *\"433. Correct Answer: A\"*.\n\n**What the two sources say, in their own words:**\n\n| Source | Wording |\n|---|---|\n| `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 21, headed **Intermediate Uveitis / Clinical pictures** | *\"**Snowballs**: aggregates of inflammatory cells in the inferior vitreous · **Snow-banking**: grey white fibrovascular plaques on the inferior pars plana\"* |\n| `ophthalmology.pdf` p.136, signs of **Intermediate Uveitis** | *\"**Snowballs:** aggregates of inflammatory cells in the inferior vitreous · **Snow banking**: grey, white fibrovascular plaques on the inferior pars plana.\"* |\n\nNeither phrase appears anywhere in either source's posterior-uveitis section. The book's posterior-uveitis signs are *\"Vitreous haziness, cells, and opacities · ± hypopyon formation\"* (p.137).\n\n**And the bank contradicts its own key on the same answer page.** `opqb-t10-421` asks what pars planitis is, keys **intermediate uveitis**, and prints a box that states: *\"**Intermediate uveitis – involves pars plana.** … **Posterior uveitis – involves retina/choroid.**\"* Snow-banking is **by definition** a plaque **on the pars plana** — so by the bank's own printed definition it cannot be a posterior-uveitis sign.\n\n**The anatomy settles it.** Both signs are named for where the material sits, and both sit in the same place:\n\n| Sign | What it is | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Snowballs** | Clumps of inflammatory cells suspended in the vitreous, drifting down under gravity | **Inferior vitreous** |\n| **Snow-banking** | Grey-white fibrovascular exudate plastered along a strip | **Inferior pars plana** — the ciliary body, i.e. the *middle* zone |\n\nThe pars plana and the anterior vitreous are precisely the territory `L13,14` slide 12 assigns to intermediate uveitis: *\"Inflammation predominantly involving the **pars plana, vitreous, and the peripheral retina**\"*. The choroid — posterior uveitis's organ — is not involved in either sign.\n\n**Why the other two distractors are wrong under any reading:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute iritis** | An **anterior**-chamber disease. Its signs are KPs, cells and flare, hypopyon, a miotic irregular pupil (Q411, Q413). Nothing in the vitreous. |\n| **Phacolytic glaucoma** | **Not a uveitis at all.** A lens-induced secondary glaucoma: a hypermature cataract leaks lens protein through an intact capsule, macrophages engorge with it and block the trabecular meshwork. `L18) Cataract II` files it under hypermature cataract — *\"MORGAGNIAN type: liquefied cortex, nucleus sinks inferiorly … Risk: **phacolytic glaucoma**\"* — and this bank asks it directly in its Lens topic. Its own \"snow\" is a white lens, not a vitreous deposit. |\n\n**How to sit an exam on this:** *snowballs and snow-banking = intermediate uveitis = pars planitis.* The three terms are one fact, and `opqb-t10-421` keys it correctly. **Answer A only if this exact question is reprinted with these exact options.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Snowballs in the inferior vitreous and snow-banking on the inferior pars plana define intermediate uveitis (pars planitis) — a point this bank keys correctly at Q421 and against here.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q433), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-434', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:"**Bruch's membrane** is part of:",
    options:['Retina','Lens capsule','Choroid','Vitreous base'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book states it outright and then, in the very next sentence, builds the trap.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.133, on the four layers of the choroid from outside in:\n\n> 1) An **outermost layer** consisting of larger diameter blood vessels. 2) Layer of medium-diameter blood vessels 3) Layer of capillaries (choriocapillaris) 4) **Bruch's membrane which is the innermost layer of the choroid.** The basement membrane of the **retinal pigment epithelium** is a part of Bruch's membrane.\n\nSo Bruch's membrane is **choroid** — its innermost lamina — and that is the answer. ⚠️ **But it is a shared border**: the RPE's own basement membrane is one of its five sub-layers, which is precisely why *\"Retina\"* is a tempting option rather than a throwaway. The book's own figure names the sub-structure — *\"RPE bm · Inner col. zone · Elastic zone · Outer col. zone · Choriocapillaris bm\"* — a sandwich with retina on one face and choroid on the other. The membrane is classified with the choroid; it is **built from both neighbours**.\n\n⚠️ **The uvea deck never mentions it.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera`'s audit records the gap: *\"The LAYERS of the iris or of the choroid (stroma, pigment epithelium, choriocapillaris, Bruch membrane). **Bruch membrane is not mentioned.**\"* Where the module does name it elsewhere, it is consistent: `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy` slide 12 labels *\"Bruch's membrane\"* between the ciliary body and *\"Choroid (on back)\"*, and `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` describes it as the structure *\"which separates the inner choroidal\"* layers from the retina.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| **Retina** | ⚠️ The real distractor. The retina's outermost layer is the **RPE**, which *sits on* Bruch's membrane. Adjacent, not the same — and the book's second sentence is what makes people choose this. |\n| **Lens capsule** | A different structure at the other end of the eye: the basement membrane of the lens epithelium, enclosing the lens. Unrelated. |\n| **Vitreous base** | The circumferential zone where the vitreous is firmly attached across the ora serrata — a **vitreoretinal** adhesion, and the reason retinal breaks cluster there. Nothing to do with the choroid. |\n\n**Why it is worth knowing this membrane by name** — three diseases in this module are defined by breaking it: **choroidal rupture** is *\"a break in Bruch's membrane\"* (the printed box at `opqb-t10-417`); **choroidal neovascularisation** grows through a defect in it, and the module lists CNV among the complications of posterior uveitis (`L13,14` slide 25); and **age-related macular degeneration** deposits drusen between the RPE and Bruch's membrane, which is where `L35,36) Chronic visual loss` locates it. A single thin lamina is the common ground of all three.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Bruch’s membrane is the innermost layer of the choroid, though the RPE basement membrane forms part of it — the interface broken in choroidal rupture, CNV and AMD.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.66 (Q434), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-435', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'In the treatment of **glaucoma secondary to iridocyclitis**, we give all these **topical** drugs **except**:',
    options:['Steroids.','Miotics.','Mydriatics.','B blockers.','Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Every drop on this list is given in a uveitic eye with a raised pressure except the miotic, and the miotic is not merely useless here — it is harmful.**\n\n**What the sources prescribe.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 give the same four-item treatment: **steroids**, **cycloplegic-mydriatics**, *\"Antiglaucoma medications in case of 2ry glaucoma **(avoid prostaglandin analogues)**\"*, and control of the cause. So options A and C are items 1 and 2 outright, and D and E are the antiglaucoma medications of item 3.\n\n| Drop | Why it is given in this eye |\n|---|---|\n| **Steroids** | Treat the cause of the pressure rise. Inflammatory cells, plasmoid aqueous and trabeculitis are what block outflow (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.135), so suppressing the inflammation is also pressure treatment. |\n| **Mydriatics** | Keep the pupil moving, breaking and preventing the posterior synechiae that can seal the pupil and produce pupil-block bombé — a **mechanical** route to secondary glaucoma (Q424, Q432). |\n| **B blockers** | Reduce aqueous **secretion** by the ciliary epithelium (Q412's structure). Effective whatever the outflow obstruction is. |\n| **Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors** | The same principle by a different enzyme — less aqueous made. |\n\n**Why a miotic is excluded, in three steps:**\n\n1. **It manufactures synechiae.** Constriction presses the pupil margin onto the anterior lens capsule and holds it there — the exact geometry in which fibrin glues iris to lens. The treatment is trying to do the opposite.\n2. **It worsens the pain.** The eye already hurts from ciliary spasm (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134: *\"brow ache (ciliary muscle spasm)\"*); a miotic contracts that muscle harder.\n3. **It increases inflammation.** Miotics increase the permeability of the blood–aqueous barrier, adding to the protein and cells already in the chamber. *(This third point is **not taken from the course material**; the first two follow directly from what the sources prescribe.)*\n\n**⚠️ The comparison that makes the rule memorable — the same drug, opposite instructions, two red painful eyes:** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists *\"Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\"* among the medical treatments of **acute angle-closure glaucoma**, where constricting the pupil pulls the peripheral iris out of the angle. In **uveitic** glaucoma the same drop is forbidden. The sign that tells them apart is the pupil — small in uveitis, mid-dilated and fixed in angle closure (Q419).\n\n**⚠️ One drug class the option list omits but the sources warn about**: *\"avoid **prostaglandin analogues**\"*. They are the modern first-line drop in open-angle glaucoma, and they are the wrong choice here — a caution the bank prints in the box at `opqb-t10-414` and then never tests.\n\n**Note the module's own silence on the mechanism.** `L12) Glaucoma 2` does not teach uveitic glaucoma at all — grepped, that deck contains no occurrence of \"uveitis\", \"inflammation\" or \"trabeculitis\". The pathophysiology this question rests on comes entirely from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135, which is where Q440 gets its options.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Uveitic secondary glaucoma is treated with steroids, mydriatics, beta-blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors; miotics are excluded because they promote synechiae and worsen ciliary spasm.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q435), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-436', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'A **middle-aged female with recurrent joint pain** gave past history of **recurrent attacks of pain, redness and dropped vision in one eye**. Recently she complained of a similar attack. The most important **diagnostic sign of activity** is:',
    options:['Festooned pupil.','Aqueous flare and cells.','Pigmented kps','Patches of iris atrophy.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Aqueous flare and cells** in the anterior chamber, detected by slit-lamp examination. **They indicate current uveal inflammation, not just past damage.**\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The question turns on one word: *activity*.** Three of the four options are **scars** — permanent records of inflammation that has already happened — and only one is a sign that the eye is inflamed **now**.\n\n| Option | Scar or activity? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Festooned pupil.** | **Scar** | Posterior synechiae that have already organised. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 pictures them as *\"irregular festooned pupil after dilatation\"*, and this bank keys them at `opqb-t10-438` as the most important sign of **chronic inactive** disease. The pupil stays festooned for life whether or not the eye is quiet. |\n| **Aqueous flare and cells.** | **ACTIVITY** | Protein and leukocytes crossing a **currently** broken blood–aqueous barrier. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 defines flare as protein *\"circulating in the aqueous secondary to inflammation and increased vascular permeability\"*; cells are *\"leukocytes in the aqueous humor … due to anterior segment inflammation with increased vascular permeability\"* (p.134). Stop the inflammation and they clear within days. |\n| **Pigmented kps** | **Scar** | ⚠️ The *pigmented* is the whole point. Fresh KPs are white and cellular; **old ones become pigmented** as they age on the endothelium, so pigmentation is a marker of past, not present, inflammation. *(The fresh-versus-pigmented distinction is **not taken from the course material** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 defines KPs only as *\"clumps of cells (WBCs) on the back surface of the cornea\"*, with no morphology, and the deck's audit records the same absence.)* Option C prints \"kps\" in lower case, as transcribed. |\n| **Patches of iris atrophy.** | **Scar** | Tissue that has been destroyed and will not return. Atrophy takes months to develop and is permanent — the record of severe or repeated past attacks. |\n\n**The vignette makes the point clinically.** *Recurrent* joint pain plus *recurrent* attacks of a red, painful eye with dropped vision is a chronic relapsing anterior uveitis on a rheumatological background — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134's *\"Connective tissue diseases\"* aetiology, which is why Q415 keys rheumatological assessment as a useful investigation. An eye like this **carries scars all the time**. If a festooned pupil or pigmented KPs counted as activity, she would appear to be in a permanent attack and would never be able to stop steroids. **Only flare and cells tell you whether to treat today.**\n\n**How it is actually looked for:** a slit-lamp beam, short and narrow, angled across the anterior chamber in a dark room — the same technique that demonstrates the Tyndall effect (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134's figure caption, *\"Cells & flare (slit-beam photograph through a dark AC showing the beam made visible)\"*). ⚠️ **Neither source grades what you see** — no SUN scale, no 0.5+ to 4+ — so the module asks only whether cells and flare are present, not how many.\n\n**The pairing to memorise:** *flare and cells = active; synechiae, pigmented KPs and iris atrophy = old.* `opqb-t10-413` and `opqb-t10-438` are the other two faces of the same distinction.",
    objective:'Aqueous flare and cells are the sign of current uveal inflammation; a festooned pupil, pigmented KPs and iris atrophy are permanent scars of past attacks and say nothing about activity.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q436), key p.70 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-437', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**64-year-old female, who had cataract surgery 3 days before**, presented with a red painful eye with decreasing vision. On examination, the eye was severely injected, severe corneal edema, **inflammatory cells and flare in the anterior chamber**, and the intraocular pressure of **40 mmhg**. The best line of treatment is:',
    options:['Antibiotic drops.','Miotics and beta-blocker drops','Steroid drops and mydriatics and beta-blocker drops.','Antibiotic and beta-blocker drops.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"This patient has **postoperative anterior uveitis with secondary ocular hypertension (high IOP 40 mmHg) following cataract surgery.** Treatment:\n\n**Steroid drops** → To control intraocular inflammation.\n\n**Mydriatics (e.g., atropine)** → Prevent posterior synechiae, relieve ciliary spasm, and help AC deepening.\n\n**Beta-blocker drops** → To lower IOP temporarily until inflammation settles.\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box's three drops are the module's standard uveitis prescription with one addition for the pressure**, and every element is on the printed lists: steroids and cycloplegic-mydriatics are items 1 and 2 of `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20 and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135, and *\"Antiglaucoma medications in case of 2ry glaucoma\"* is item 3. Both sources name **ocular surgery** as an aetiology of uveitis in the first place (`L13,14` slide 11, *\"Ocular surgery\"*; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.134, *\"Following ocular surgery\"*), so a postoperative anterior uveitis is a taught entity, not an improvisation.\n\n**Why the pressure is 40.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 gives the two mechanisms by which uveitis raises rather than lowers the IOP: *\"**Trabeculitis** … **Plasmoid aqueous and circulating large-sized WBCs obstruct the trabecular meshwork** resulting in impaired aqueous drainage.\"* After cataract surgery a third can be added — retained viscoelastic — which `L18) Cataract II` lists among early postoperative problems (*\"retained viscoelastic, raised IOP\"*). The beta-blocker is a holding measure while the steroid removes the cause.\n\n**⚠️ THE CROSS-REFERENCE THIS ENTRY EXISTS TO MAKE, AND IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE KEY.** The same bank, in its Lens topic, prints a near neighbour of this vignette — `opqb-t9-355`: *\"After 48 hours of cataract extraction operation, a patient complained of ocular pain and visual loss. On examination, this eye looked red with ciliary injection, corneal oedema and absent red reflex. The first suspicion must be:\"* — and keys **bacterial endophthalmitis**, with a printed box saying *\"Postoperative red eye, pain, corneal edema, absent red reflex → suspect endophthalmitis first.\"* **Two questions, the same window after surgery, opposite answers.** What separates them in the printed text:\n\n| | `opqb-t9-355` | This question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interval | 48 hours | 3 days |\n| Red reflex | **ABSENT** — the vitreous is full of something | not mentioned |\n| Vitreous | implied involved | not mentioned |\n| **Hypopyon** | not stated | **not stated** |\n| Pressure | not stated | **40 mmHg** |\n| Keyed as | **Endophthalmitis — first suspicion** | Postoperative uveitis |\n\n**The clinically safe reading, and it is worth more than either key:** an eye 3 days after cataract surgery with pain, falling vision and anterior-chamber cells is **acute postoperative endophthalmitis until proved otherwise**, and the discriminators are a **hypopyon**, a **vitreous** reaction and a **lost red reflex**. `L18) Cataract II` gives the response — *\"urgent **vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)**. Severe: **vitrectomy**\"* — and note that **topical antibiotic drops are not the treatment of endophthalmitis either**, which is why option A is wrong under both readings. **Answer C as this bank prints it; in a real eye, exclude endophthalmitis first.**\n\n**Why the other three fail on the bank's own reading:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Antibiotic drops.** | Drops do not penetrate to the vitreous, so they treat neither endophthalmitis nor the inflammation described, and nothing is done about a pressure of 40. |\n| **Miotics and beta-blocker drops** | ⚠️ The miotic again — contraindicated in an inflamed eye (Q418, Q435, Q441), and here it would also worsen the pain of a spastic ciliary body. |\n| **Antibiotic and beta-blocker drops.** | Lowers the pressure but leaves the inflammation entirely untreated, and adds an antibiotic with no target. |\n\n**⚠️ The stem prints \"40 mmhg\" in lower case; transcribed as printed.**",
    objective:'Postoperative anterior uveitis with a raised IOP is treated with a steroid, a mydriatic and a beta-blocker — but a painful eye days after cataract surgery must first be cleared of endophthalmitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q437), key p.70 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-438', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The most important sign of **chronic inactive iridocyclitis** is:',
    options:['Aqueous flare.','Posterior synechiae.','Corneal edema.','Patch of iris atrophy.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**This is `opqb-t10-436` turned around: that question asked for the sign of activity, this one asks what an eye that has *finished* being inflamed looks like.**\n\n**Posterior synechiae are the answer because they are the permanent, visible record of inflammation, and both sources list them first among the sequelae.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 18: *\"Anterior uveitis / **Sequelae and complications** — **Posterior synechiae** · Secondary glaucoma\"*, illustrated with *\"two clinical photographs of pupils grossly distorted into festooned/irregular shapes\"*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 heads its complication list the same way and pictures *\"Posterior synechiae ×2 (irregular festooned pupil after dilatation)\"*.\n\n**What they are and why they persist.** Fibrinous exudate between the posterior iris and the anterior lens capsule organises into fibrous adhesion. Once organised it does not resolve: the iris stays tethered, so the pupil is permanently irregular and dilates in petals — the **festooned** pupil. Nothing about that appearance depends on whether the eye is inflamed today, which is exactly what makes it the sign of *past* disease. `L37) Red Eye` gives the same definition: *\"posterior synechiae, which are **inflammatory adhesions between the lens and the iris**\"*.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Aqueous flare.** | ⚠️ The direct opposite — this is the sign of **ACTIVE** inflammation, keyed as such by this bank at `opqb-t10-436` (*\"They indicate current uveal inflammation, not just past damage\"*) and as the earliest feature at `opqb-t10-413`. In an *inactive* eye the flare has settled. ⚠️ *(One qualification, **not taken from the course material**: after very long-standing disease a low-grade flare can persist from permanent barrier damage without cells — but cells, not flare, are what define activity, and the module does not teach the distinction.)* |\n| **Corneal edema.** | An **acute** sign, and mostly of a different disease. Corneal oedema in a red painful eye points to acute **angle-closure glaucoma** (`L12) Glaucoma 2`: *\"Corneal oedema\"* among its signs) or to a very high pressure or endothelial failure (`opqb-t10-437`). A quiet, chronic eye has a clear cornea — or, if uveitis has been chronic for years, **band keratopathy**, which is calcium in a horizontal band rather than oedema. |\n| **Patch of iris atrophy.** | ⚠️ The strongest distractor, and it is a genuine scar — but a **less common and less specific** one. Sectoral iris atrophy is the hallmark of **herpetic** anterior uveitis rather than of chronic iridocyclitis in general; most quiet post-uveitic eyes show synechiae and no atrophy. The question asks for the **most important** sign, and that is the one nearly every such eye has. *(The herpetic association is **not taken from the course material** — the module names HSV and HZV as causes of uveitis but describes no clinical picture for either.)* |\n\n**Why the sign matters practically.** A festooned pupil in a quiet white eye tells you three things at once: this eye **has had uveitis**, it may relapse, and it is at risk of **pupil block** if the adhesions ever complete a full circle — the chain that ends in iris bombé and secondary glaucoma (`opqb-t10-424`, `opqb-t10-432`). It also explains a poor dilation at every future examination, which matters when the fundus needs to be seen.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Posterior synechiae — a permanently festooned pupil — are the sign of chronic inactive iridocyclitis, where flare marks activity, corneal oedema marks acute high pressure and iris atrophy is less common.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q438), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-439', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The **early diagnosis** of acute iridocyclitis depends on the following **except**:',
    options:['Ciliary injection.','Small sluggish pupil','Presence of aqueous flare and cells in the anterior chamber.','Presence of keratic precipitates.','Presence of lens edema.'],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**Four of these are the module's sign list for anterior uveitis, in order. The fifth is not a sign of anything.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` pp.134–135 and `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slides 14–17 between them print exactly the first four:\n\n| Option | Where it is printed |\n|---|---|\n| **Ciliary injection.** | *\"**Perilimbal (ciliary) injection** with violaceous hue\"* (book p.134); `L13,14` slide 14 photographs it. |\n| **Small sluggish pupil** | *\"**Miosis** (an early sign) with sluggish pupillary light reaction\"* (book p.134) — the sign `opqb-t10-428` keys as the early one. |\n| **Aqueous flare and cells** | Book pp.134–135, both defined; `L13,14` slide 16, *\"Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± hypopyon\"*. The earliest feature of all (`opqb-t10-413`). |\n| **Keratic precipitates.** | *\"**Keratic precipitates (KPs)**: formation of clumps of cells (WBCs) on the back surface of the cornea\"* (book p.135); `L13,14` slide 15. |\n\n**⚠️ \"Lens edema\" is not a taught entity — the absence is measured.** Grepped case-insensitively across all 29 lecture caches and all 12 cached book chapters, the module never pairs *lens* with *oedema/edema* as a clinical sign. **The lens does not become oedematous in uveitis.** What actually happens to a lens in chronic uveitis is **complicated cataract** — an opacity, listed as a complication by both sources (`L13,14` slide 19, *\"Complicated Cataract\"*; book p.135) — and that develops over months, so it could never serve an *early* diagnosis either. Option E fails twice: wrong process, wrong timescale. *(That lens opacification rather than oedema is the uveitic lens change is the sources' own wording; the statement that no such sign exists is a measured absence, not a citation.)*\n\n**⚠️ A precision worth noting about the other four, recorded rather than disputed.** The question asks what **early** diagnosis depends on, and **keratic precipitates are not the earliest** of the four — cells must first enter the chamber and then clump and settle on the endothelium, which is why `opqb-t10-413` keys **aqueous flare** rather than KPs as the earliest feature. But KPs are still an early *diagnostic* sign in the sense the question means — visible at the first slit-lamp examination in a typical acute attack — and option E is so plainly not a sign at all that the key is unambiguous.\n\n**The examination technique behind the list:** these are four things one torch and one slit lamp will show in under a minute — **look at the redness and where it is maximal** (limbal, not fornix-based), **look at the pupil** (small, irregular, sluggish), **run a narrow beam through the chamber in the dark** (flare and cells), **and look at the back of the cornea** (KPs). `L37) Red Eye` calls the pupil finding a triage sign: *\"a pupillary inequality or irregularity in a patient with red eye(s) is a **danger signal** for serious ocular disease.\"*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Early diagnosis of acute iridocyclitis rests on ciliary injection, a small sluggish pupil, flare and cells, and keratic precipitates; lens oedema is not a sign the module teaches at all.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q439), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-440', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**Secondary glaucoma due to iridocyclitis** may be due to the following **except**:',
    options:['Increased formation of the watery aqueous.','Obstruction of the trabecular meshwork by the inflammatory exudates.','Organization of inflammatory exudates results in formation of P.A.S.','Pupilary block by posterior synechine.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Inflammation of the ciliary body makes the eye produce LESS aqueous, not more — so \"increased formation\" is the one mechanism that cannot raise the pressure here.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 states both directions in one block, and the first line is the answer:\n\n> **IOP:** – **Usually decreases** due to ciliary body inflammation with **decreased aqueous production (ciliary body shutdown)** – Sometimes the IOP increases [Secondary inflammatory glaucoma] due to: • **Trabeculitis** as in HSV or HZV iritis • **Plasmoid aqueous and circulating large-sized WBCs obstruct the trabecular meshwork** resulting in impaired aqueous drainage\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 17 compresses the same fact to a phrase: *\"IOP: usually **decreased** but may be normal or increased\"* — the point `opqb-t10-447` is built on.\n\n**The principle: every real mechanism here is an OUTFLOW problem.** Pressure = inflow minus outflow, and inflammation reduces inflow. So every route to a raised pressure in this eye must block the drain:\n\n| Option | Where the blockage is | Reversible? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Obstruction of the trabecular meshwork by the inflammatory exudates.** | **Inside the meshwork** — plasmoid aqueous, protein and swollen white cells clog the filter. The book's own second mechanism. | **Yes** — clears as the inflammation settles, which is why a beta-blocker is a *temporary* measure (`opqb-t10-437`). |\n| **Organization of inflammatory exudates results in formation of P.A.S.** | **Across the angle** — exudate organises into fibrous tissue that sticks the peripheral iris onto the meshwork: **peripheral anterior synechiae**, the \"PAS\" of `opqb-t10-423`. | **No** — an anatomical closure, permanent, and the reason chronic uveitis can need fistulising surgery (`opqb-t10-416`). |\n| **Pupilary block by posterior synechine.** | **At the pupil** — 360° adhesion of iris to lens seals the pupil, aqueous cannot reach the anterior chamber, the iris balloons forward as **iris bombé** and closes the angle. `L12) Glaucoma 2` prints the chain: *relative pupil block → accumulation of aqueous humour behind the iris → iris bombe → narrowing of the angle*. | Relieved by **iridotomy** (`opqb-t10-424`, `opqb-t10-432`). |\n\n⚠️ **Option D carries two misprints in one line**: the bank prints **\"Pupilary\"** for *pupillary* and **\"synechine\"** for *synechiae*. Both are transcribed exactly as printed.\n\n**Why option A is not simply unlisted but impossible in this disease.** The ciliary epithelium is both the inflamed tissue and the secreting tissue (`opqb-t10-412`). When it is inflamed it **shuts down**, which is why the classic finding in acute iridocyclitis is a **low** pressure. An eye cannot simultaneously have a ciliary body too inflamed to work and one working harder than normal. There is no clinical state of \"increased formation of watery aqueous\" in uveitis at all. *(The phrase \"watery aqueous\" is the bank's own; it presumably contrasts with the *plasmoid* aqueous the book names — thick, protein-rich aqueous — which is itself part of mechanism B.)*\n\n**The one raised-pressure mechanism the option list leaves out**, and it matters in practice: **steroid response**. The drug used to treat the uveitis can itself raise the pressure — the bank names it at `opqb-t10-447` (*\"if trabeculitis, PAS formation, or **steroid response** develops\"*), though neither the deck nor the book chapter ever mentions steroid-induced glaucoma. A pressure that rises three weeks into treatment is a different problem from one that is high on day one.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Uveitic secondary glaucoma is always an outflow problem — trabecular clogging, PAS, or pupil block from synechiae — because the inflamed ciliary body produces less aqueous, not more.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q440), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-441', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**Increase of the ocular tension during the acute attack of iridocyclitis** is managed by the following **except**:',
    options:['Corticosteroids and atropine.','Beta-blockers.','Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.','Miotics'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The third framing of the same rule in this topic, and the reason it is asked three times is that the reflex answer is wrong every time.** `opqb-t10-418` forbids pilocarpine in the treatment of the attack, `opqb-t10-435` excludes miotics from the topical drugs used for uveitic glaucoma, and this question excludes them from the management of the pressure spike itself.\n\n**Why the other three work, and note that they attack the problem from two different directions:**\n\n| Option | Direction of attack |\n|---|---|\n| **Corticosteroids and atropine.** | **Treat the cause.** The pressure is high because inflammatory debris is clogging the meshwork and because synechiae are forming; steroids clear the debris and atropine breaks the adhesions. Both sources give this pair as items 1 and 2 of the treatment (`L13,14` slide 20; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135). It is the only option here that shortens the disease rather than managing a number. |\n| **Beta-blockers.** | **Reduce inflow** — less aqueous secreted by the ciliary epithelium. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 item 3: *\"Antiglaucoma medications in case of 2ry glaucoma (avoid prostaglandin analogues)\"*, and `L12) Glaucoma 2` lists *\"Topical beta-blockers\"* among pressure-lowering drugs. |\n| **Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.** | **Reduce inflow** by a different enzyme. `L12` lists both *\"Systemic acetazolamide\"* and *\"Topical CAIs\"*. |\n\n**Why a miotic cannot be used, even though the pressure is high.** This is the trap, and it is a good one: a miotic *is* a pressure-lowering drug — in **primary angle closure**, where `L12) Glaucoma 2` prescribes *\"Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\"* precisely to pull the peripheral iris out of a crowded angle. In **uveitis** the same drug is contraindicated because:\n\n1. **It presses the pupil onto the lens**, manufacturing the posterior synechiae that are one of the mechanisms raising the pressure in the first place (`opqb-t10-440`, option D).\n2. **It contracts a ciliary muscle already in painful spasm** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134: *\"brow ache (ciliary muscle spasm)\"*).\n3. **It increases blood–aqueous barrier permeability**, adding protein and cells to a chamber that already has too many. *(This third point is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**And in pupil-block bombé it would be worse than useless.** When the pupil is already sealed by 360° synechiae, constricting it cannot open anything — the block is at the pupil margin. The treatment there is mechanical: a **peripheral iridotomy** (`opqb-t10-416`, `opqb-t10-432`).\n\n**The rule in one line, and it is the single most transferable fact in this topic:** *a red painful eye with a high pressure gets a miotic only if it is angle closure; if it is uveitis, the miotic is the one drug you must not give.* The sign that decides is the **pupil** — mid-dilated and fixed in angle closure, small and irregular in uveitis (`opqb-t10-419`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A pressure spike in acute iridocyclitis is managed with steroids and atropine plus beta-blockers or carbonic anhydrase inhibitors; miotics are contraindicated despite lowering pressure in angle closure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q441), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-442', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'**Corticosteroids** are sight saving in the management of **acute uveitis**, the best form is;',
    options:['Topical drops because it reaches in high concentration.','Systemic because the uveal tract is highly vascular','Intra vitreal injection.','Combination of topical and systemic.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**⚠️ NEITHER SOURCE RANKS THE ROUTES — they list them, and the ranking in the key is the bank's own.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 20 prints *\"**Steroids (Topical ± periocular ± systemic)**\"*; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 prints *\"**Steroids (topical - periocular - Systemic)**\"*. Both use a neutral connector — plus-or-minus, or a dash — and neither says which is best. The reasoning below is therefore **not taken from the course material** except where a source is named.\n\n**What each route can and cannot reach — and this is why a combination is defensible:**\n\n| Route | Reaches | Does not reach |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Topical drops** | **Cornea, anterior chamber, iris, ciliary body** — high local concentration exactly where anterior uveitis lives, with almost no systemic exposure | The **posterior** segment: drops do not achieve useful vitreous or choroidal levels |\n| **Periocular injection** | A depot behind the eye giving sustained posterior levels — the route both sources list second, and the one this option menu leaves out entirely | — |\n| **Systemic** | **Everywhere**, including the choroid and the systemic disease that may be driving the uveitis | — but at the price of systemic toxicity |\n| **Intravitreal** | The **vitreous cavity** directly, in high concentration | Nothing else; and it is an intraocular procedure with real risks |\n\n**Why option D is keyed.** In a **severe acute uveitis**, drops control the anterior chamber where the cells and flare are, while systemic steroid covers what drops cannot — any posterior or intermediate component (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.136: intermediate uveitis is *\"Associated with anterior uveitis in about 50 % of cases\"*), and the underlying systemic disease. The stem's word **\"sight saving\"** signals the severe end of the spectrum, where a single route is left doing a job it cannot finish. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.138 states the general principle the choice serves: *\"**Corticosteroids are the mainstay of therapy in treating noninfectious causes.**\"*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong as stated:**\n\n| Option | The flaw |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical drops because it reaches in high concentration.** | The *reason* is right and the *scope* is wrong. Drops do achieve high anterior concentration — and for an ordinary, isolated, mild-to-moderate **anterior** uveitis they are usually enough. But they cannot treat a posterior component, and the stem is describing sight-threatening disease. |\n| **Systemic because the uveal tract is highly vascular** | Again a true premise — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.131 opens by calling the uvea *\"the **vascular middle layer** of the eye\"* — supporting the wrong conclusion. Systemic steroid alone under-treats the anterior chamber compared with drops applied directly to it, and carries the whole systemic burden. |\n| **Intra vitreal injection.** | Reserved for resistant posterior or intermediate disease, not a first choice in acute uveitis. The book lists intravitreal steroid only in the **intermediate** and **posterior** treatment rows of its p.140 table, never for anterior uveitis. An intraocular injection also carries the risk this topic keys elsewhere — endophthalmitis (`opqb-t10-445`). |\n\n**⚠️ The stem ends with a semicolon where a colon is meant (\"the best form is;\") — transcribed as printed.**\n\n**The caution neither source prints but every prescriber needs**: steroids in the eye cause **steroid-induced glaucoma** and **steroid cataract**, and steroids given before infection is excluded can be disastrous — which is the one warning the book *does* print (p.138): *\"Although infection is an uncommon cause of uveitis, it is important to **rule it out before instituting immunosuppressive therapy**.\"* The deck's own audit records the rest of the gap: *\"Steroid COMPLICATIONS (steroid-induced glaucoma, steroid cataract) as a caution. **Absent.**\"*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In sight-threatening acute uveitis the bank keys topical plus systemic steroid — drops for the anterior chamber, systemic cover for posterior and systemic disease — though neither source ranks the routes.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.67 (Q442), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-443', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'A **female patient 55 years of age previously emmetropic** is complaining of right defective vision. On examination the vision was **hand movement**. The **lens was completely opacified**. On dilatation the **pupil was festooned** and the **tension was 2 mm Hg**. On ultrasonography the **axial length was 20 mm**. This lady must have a history of:',
    options:['Primary angle closure glaucoma','Mature senile cataract.','Anterior uveitis','Corneal ulcer.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Three findings in this vignette point at one disease, and only one option accounts for all three.**\n\n| Finding | What it means | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Festooned pupil** | **Posterior synechiae** — iris permanently adherent to the anterior lens capsule. The signature of past anterior uveitis, keyed by this bank at `opqb-t10-438` as the sign of chronic inactive iridocyclitis. | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 pictures *\"Posterior synechiae ×2 (irregular festooned pupil after dilatation)\"*; `L13,14` slide 18 photographs *\"pupils grossly distorted into festooned/irregular shapes\"* |\n| **Tension 2 mm Hg** | **Hypotony** — the ciliary body has stopped secreting. | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135: IOP *\"**Usually decreases** due to ciliary body inflammation with **decreased aqueous production (ciliary body shutdown)**\"*; `L13,14` slide 17: *\"IOP: usually decreased\"* |\n| **Axial length 20 mm in a previously emmetropic eye** | The globe has **shrunk**. An emmetropic adult eye is around 23–24 mm; 20 mm means the eye has lost volume — a pre-phthisical eye. *(The normal axial length figure is **not taken from the course material**; `L17) Cataract I` and `L18) Cataract II` name axial length only as a biometry measurement for IOL power.)* |\n\nTogether these describe an eye burnt out by **long-standing anterior uveitis**: synechiae from the inflammation, a **complicated cataract** (both sources list it as a complication — `L13,14` slide 19, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135), and a ciliary body so damaged it no longer maintains the pressure or the volume of the globe.\n\n**Why the other three fail — each explains at most one finding:**\n\n| Option | What it explains, and what it cannot |\n|---|---|\n| **Primary angle closure glaucoma** | Would leave a **high** or previously high pressure, a **mid-dilated, vertically oval, fixed** pupil (`L12) Glaucoma 2`), and possibly glaukomflecken — **not** a festooned pupil and **certainly not a pressure of 2**. Wrong direction on the one number given. |\n| **Mature senile cataract.** | ⚠️ The tempting option, because the lens really is completely opacified and `L18) Cataract II` defines mature cataract as *\"VA: **HM only (not less!)** · Red Reflex: **ABSENT** · Totally opaque white lens\"* — the vision matches exactly. **But senile cataract explains nothing else.** It does not bind the pupil, it does not cause hypotony, and it does not shrink the globe. It is the *consequence* here, not the history the question asks for. |\n| **Corneal ulcer.** | A corneal disease. A healed ulcer leaves a **corneal scar** — and the examination in this stem describes a clear enough cornea to see a dilated pupil and an opacified lens through. It also does not produce hypotony or a short eye. |\n\n**The reasoning pattern worth taking away:** when a vignette gives several apparently unrelated numbers, find the **single disease that produces all of them**. Here the pupil says inflammation, the pressure says ciliary body failure, and the axial length says the failure has been going on for years. Cataract, angle closure and corneal ulcer each account for one line of the stem and contradict another.\n\n**This vignette continues into `opqb-t10-444`**, which asks what to do about the cataract — and the answer there depends entirely on recognising the eye described here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A festooned pupil, hypotony and a shortened globe in an eye with a white lens describe an eye burnt out by long-standing anterior uveitis, of which the cataract is a complication.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.68 (Q443), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-444', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The best way of managing the above case — the **55-year-old previously emmetropic woman** whose right eye sees **hand movements**, with a **completely opacified lens**, a **festooned pupil**, an intraocular pressure of **2 mm Hg** and an **axial length of 20 mm** — is:',
    options:['Extracapsular cataract extraction with IOL implantation.','Extracapsular cataract extraction without IOL.','No surgery is indicated.','Intracapsular cataract extraction.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ The bank prints only \"The best way of managing the above case is:\". The antecedent is `opqb-t10-443`, immediately above it on book p.68, and its findings are restated here so the entry stands alone. The restatement carries the hypotony and the short axial length because those are the whole reason for the answer, and deliberately omits the diagnosis, which is what Q443 asks for.)*\n\n**Cataract surgery is done to restore vision. In this eye it cannot, and it would probably make things worse — so the answer is to leave it alone.**\n\n**Why the eye is beyond restoring.** A pressure of 2 mm Hg and an axial length of 20 mm in an eye that was emmetropic mean the ciliary body has failed and the globe is shrinking: this is a **pre-phthisical eye** after years of uveitis. The lens opacity is one symptom of a globe that has stopped working, not the obstacle in front of a healthy retina. Removing it would leave the same failed ciliary body, the same hypotony and the same shrinkage — with an added surgical insult. *(The concept of phthisis bulbi is **not taken from the course material** — `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera`'s audit lists *\"PHTHISIS BULBI and CYCLITIC MEMBRANE as sequelae of uveitis. **Absent.**\"* — but every finding it rests on is printed by the sources, as set out at `opqb-t10-443`.)*\n\n**Why operating would actively harm it.** Two things the module does teach make the point:\n\n1. **Surgery reignites uveitis.** Both sources name *\"Following ocular surgery\"* / *\"Ocular surgery\"* as a **cause** of uveitis (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134; `L13,14` slide 11), and an eye with a history of chronic uveitis is the one most likely to flare — the picture `opqb-t10-437` describes.\n2. **A hypotonous eye tolerates surgery badly.** Opening a globe already at 2 mm Hg risks choroidal effusion and haemorrhage and can tip a pre-phthisical eye into frank phthisis. *(Not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**How the module tells you when NOT to operate.** `L18) Cataract II` gives the prognostic tests done **before** cataract surgery precisely to answer this question: *\"**Projection of light** — Good in all 4 directions = good prognosis\"* and *\"**Color discrimination** — Red vs green test = good retinal function\"*, alongside biometry (*\"Axial length (IOL Master/Lenstar preferred)\"*). Those tests exist because a white lens is not by itself an indication. This bank asks the same principle from the other side in its Lens topic — *\"A bad prognosis for visual improvement is expected for cataract extraction if:\"*.\n\n**Why the three surgical options are all wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Extracapsular cataract extraction with IOL implantation.** | The standard operation for a normal eye — and biometry on a 20 mm globe would in any case give an unreliable lens power. It restores nothing here. |\n| **Extracapsular cataract extraction without IOL.** | Leaves the patient **aphakic** — a +10 D correction and aniseikonia — in exchange for no visual gain, since the problem is behind the lens. |\n| **Intracapsular cataract extraction.** | The most traumatic technique of the three, removing lens and capsule together — `L18) Cataract II` lists it as *\"ICCE — Intracapsular Extraction\"* against phaco as *\"STANDARD\"*, and records its consequence, *\"ICCE (historical); posterior IOL\"* problems. The worst choice for the most fragile eye. |\n\n**The general rule this pair of questions teaches:** *a dense cataract is an indication for surgery only when the eye behind it can see.* Test the projection of light, test colour, measure the pressure, and ask why the eye became cataractous in the first place.\n\n*(Filed under Uvea with `opqb-t10-443`, of which it is the second half; secondary chapter: cataract surgery.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Cataract surgery is not indicated in a hypotonous, shrinking eye blinded by chronic uveitis — the lens is a symptom of the failed globe, and operating risks flare, effusion and frank phthisis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.68 (Q444), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-445', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'In a patient presenting with **acute red eye, diminution of vision, anterior chamber flare and cells and keratic precipitates on the back of the cornea**, all of the following statements regarding the **aetiology** of the condition are correct **EXCEPT**:',
    options:["A male sex together with a history of painful oral and genital ulcers highly suggest the diagnosis of Behcet's disease",'A tender globe together with a hypopyon highly suggest the diagnosis of an infectious origin','A female sex together with a vitiligo and poliosis highly suggest the diagnosis of Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada disease','A history of prior cataract extraction suggests the possibility of Endophthalmitis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The stem describes an anterior uveitis** — flare, cells and keratic precipitates are the book's own sign list (`ophthalmology.pdf` pp.134–135) — **and then offers four aetiological clues, three of which are genuine pointers and one of which is a trap.**\n\n**Why the keyed exception is B.** A **hypopyon in an eye with anterior uveitis is far more often sterile than infectious.** The book treats it as an ordinary sign of the disease, listing it among the signs of anterior uveitis without any infectious qualifier (p.134: *\"**Hypopyon (pus in the AC)**: it represents a collection of neutrophils and exudates in the inferior part of the AC\"*) — and it gives the commonest aetiology of anterior uveitis as **idiopathic, 75 % of cases**. The classic hypopyon uveitis is **Behçet's**, which is option A's disease, and HLA-B27 anterior uveitis (option A of the aetiology list at `opqb-t10-427`) also produces a fibrinous hypopyon. So a hypopyon does not \"highly suggest an infectious origin\" — it suggests severe inflammation of any cause. *(The sterile-hypopyon point is **not taken from the course material** beyond the book's own unqualified listing of hypopyon as a sign; the deck's audit confirms *\"No hypopyon-vs-pseudohypopyon distinction; no Behçet hypopyon description beyond the aetiology line.\"*)*\n\n**⚠️ And note what \"tender globe\" adds — the module attaches globe tenderness to two things, neither of them infection:** anterior uveitis itself (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.134: *\"Ocular pain with **globe tenderness** and brow ache\"*) and **scleritis** (`L13,14` slide 34: *\"Moderate to severe pain with **globe tenderness**\"*). The option pairs two non-specific findings and draws a specific conclusion from them.\n\n**Why the other three are correct as stated:**\n\n| Option | The pointer, and where the module supports it |\n|---|---|\n| **Behçet's disease** — oral and genital ulcers | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.137 names it and its triad in one line: *\"Autoimmune e.g., **Behçet's disease (oral ulcers, genital ulcers, and posterior uveitis)**\"*; `L13,14` slide 11 lists it among immune-mediated causes. ⚠️ **The name is spelled three ways across the module** — the bank prints \"Behcet's\", the deck prints \"Bechet's\", the book prints \"Behçet's\". |\n| **VKH** — vitiligo and poliosis | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.140, criterion 5: *\"Affection of the skin and its adnexa e.g., **vitiligo, alopecia and poliosis**\"*. This is the disease `opqb-t10-431` is built on. |\n| **Endophthalmitis** after cataract extraction | Both sources name **ocular surgery** as an aetiology of uveitis, and `L18) Cataract II` names the specific entity: *\"**24–72 hrs** (acute) or weeks (delayed) · CoNS (**Staphylococcus epidermidis** most common) · Signs: **pain, redness, reduced VA, hypopyon, yellowish vitreous haze** · Tx: urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics.\"* A history of intraocular surgery genuinely does raise it. |\n\n**⚠️ The internal tension worth seeing.** Option D says a hypopyon **after surgery** should make you think of infection, and that is correct — while option B says a hypopyon **in general** implies infection, and that is not. The difference is the **context**, not the sign: hypopyon plus recent intraocular surgery is endophthalmitis until proved otherwise (which is the reading `opqb-t10-437` should be held against), whereas hypopyon in a young man with mouth ulcers is Behçet's. **The sign is the same; the history decides.** That is the whole lesson of this question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Oral and genital ulcers point to Behçet, vitiligo and poliosis to VKH, and recent cataract surgery to endophthalmitis; a hypopyon with a tender globe alone does not imply infection.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.68 (Q445), key p.70' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-446', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'The **differential diagnosis of acute iridocyclitis** includes the following conditions **except**:',
    options:['Corneal ulcer','Open angle glaucoma','Acute conjunctivitis','Angle closure glaucoma'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Acute iridocyclitis (anterior uveitis) can mimic several **red-eye conditions,** but **open-angle glaucoma** is not typically confused with acute uveitis, because **it usually presents painlessly and with gradual visual field loss, not acute redness, pain, or AC reaction.**\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The differential diagnosis of acute iridocyclitis is the differential diagnosis of the ACUTE RED EYE, and chronic open-angle glaucoma is not a red eye at all.** `L37) Red Eye` says so in one sentence: *\"The more common **chronic open-angle glaucoma causes no redness of the eye.**\"* `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` teaches it as an insidious, painless, symptomless disease found on screening or by field loss — which is precisely why it is dangerous, and precisely why it cannot be mistaken for an eye that hurts.\n\n**The three that ARE in the differential, and the sign that separates each from iridocyclitis** — all four rows from `L37) Red Eye`:\n\n| Condition | How it resembles iridocyclitis | What separates it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Corneal ulcer** | Red, painful, photophobic, reduced vision — and a significant ulcer causes a **secondary anterior uveitis**, which is why `L15,16) Cornea` prescribes *\"cycloplegic drops: reduce pain from ciliary spasm, prevent synechiae\"* for bacterial keratitis | **Fluorescein.** `L37`: *\"areas of bright-green staining denote absent or diseased epithelium\"*. The lesion is visible on the cornea; in pure iridocyclitis the cornea stains nowhere |\n| **Acute conjunctivitis** | Red eye, discomfort, watering | **Everything else.** `L37`: reduced acuity *\"never occurs in simple conjunctivitis unless there is associated corneal involvement\"*; ciliary flush *\"is not present in conjunctivitis\"*; *\"Conjunctivitis does not affect the pupil.\"* Redness is maximal in the **fornices**, not at the limbus, and there is **discharge** |\n| **Angle closure glaucoma** | Red, very painful, vision down, ciliary injection — the closest mimic of all | **The pupil and the pressure.** Mid-dilated, vertically oval, fixed, with a **stony hard** eye and a shallow chamber (`L12) Glaucoma 2`), against a small irregular sluggish pupil and a usually **low** pressure in iridocyclitis. `opqb-t10-419` makes this the whole question |\n\n**Why the distinction matters more than most differentials:** these four conditions take **incompatible treatments**. Iridocyclitis gets a steroid and a **dilator**; angle closure gets a **constrictor** and urgent pressure lowering; a bacterial ulcer gets intensive **antibiotics** and steroids may be harmful; conjunctivitis usually needs nothing but hygiene. Getting the wrong one is not a labelling error — it is the wrong drug in an eye that has hours to lose.\n\n**The bedside triage `L37) Red Eye` builds this on:** measure **acuity**, look at **where** the redness is (limbal flush versus fornix), stain with **fluorescein**, look at the **pupil**, estimate the **chamber depth**, and measure the **pressure**. *\"IOP is unaffected by common causes of red eye other than iridocyclitis (IOP often low) and glaucoma (IOP often elevated)\"* — a single tonometry reading separates the two most serious members of the list, in opposite directions.",
    objective:'Acute iridocyclitis is differentiated among the acute red eyes — corneal ulcer, acute conjunctivitis, angle closure — while chronic open-angle glaucoma is painless, white and found by field loss.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.68 (Q446), key p.70 (explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t10-447', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'In **acute iridocyclitis**, the intraocular pressure is:',
    options:['Increased','Decreased','Normal','Any of the above'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"intraocular pressure (IOP) in acute iridocyclitis can vary depending on the stage and severity:\n\n**Decreased IOP** → Most common early in acute inflammation due to ciliary body shutdown.\n\n**Normal IOP** → Mild cases or between attacks.\n\n**Increased IOP** → Can occur if trabeculitis, PAS formation, or steroid response develops.\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box is the deck's one-line teaching expanded, and the book's paragraph condensed.** `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 17: *\"**IOP: usually decreased but may be normal or increased**\"*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.135 gives the mechanisms on both sides:\n\n> **IOP:** – **Usually decreases** due to ciliary body inflammation with decreased aqueous production (**ciliary body shutdown**) – Sometimes the IOP increases [Secondary inflammatory glaucoma] due to: • **Trabeculitis** as in HSV or HZV iritis • **Plasmoid aqueous and circulating large-sized WBCs obstruct the trabecular meshwork**\n\n**The physiology in one sentence: pressure is inflow minus outflow, and uveitis attacks both.**\n\n| Direction | Mechanism | Where the module states it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Down** (commonest) | The inflamed **ciliary body stops secreting** — inflow falls | Book p.135, *\"ciliary body shutdown\"*; deck slide 17 |\n| **Up** | Outflow blocked — **trabeculitis**, **plasmoid aqueous and swollen white cells** clogging the meshwork, **PAS** sealing the angle, **pupil block** from 360° synechiae | Book p.135; `opqb-t10-440` sets out all four |\n| **Up, late** | **Steroid response** to the treatment itself | ⚠️ Named in the printed box above and **nowhere else in the module** — the deck's audit records *\"Steroid COMPLICATIONS (steroid-induced glaucoma, steroid cataract) as a caution. **Absent.**\"* |\n| **Normal** | The two effects cancel, or the disease is mild | Box above |\n\n**Why \"any of the above\" is the honest answer and not a cop-out.** It is the only option that survives the whole clinical course. An eye can be hypotonous on day one, normal on day five, and hypertensive at three weeks on steroids — **the same eye, the same disease, three different pressures.** A question that asked \"usually\" would key **decreased**; this one asks what the pressure *is*, and the answer is that it depends on stage and severity, which is exactly what the box says.\n\n**Why the three single answers are each wrong on their own:**\n\n| Option | Why not alone |\n|---|---|\n| **Increased** | True in secondary inflammatory glaucoma, but the *less* common direction — and treating every uveitic eye as hypertensive is how a low pressure gets missed. |\n| **Decreased** | The commonest and the classic — **but treating it as invariable is how a pressure of 40 gets missed**, which is the eye at `opqb-t10-437`. |\n| **Normal** | Common in mild disease and between attacks, and reassuring precisely because it is not diagnostic. |\n\n**The clinical instruction that follows:** **measure the pressure in every eye with uveitis, at every visit** — including visits during treatment. It is the one number in this disease that can move in both directions and can do so under the influence of the drug being used to treat it. And note the contrast with the differential at `opqb-t10-446`: `L37) Red Eye` says *\"IOP is unaffected by common causes of red eye other than **iridocyclitis (IOP often low)** and **glaucoma (IOP often elevated)**\"* — which makes tonometry both the test that separates them and the test whose result, in uveitis, cannot be predicted.",
    objective:'IOP in acute iridocyclitis may be low (ciliary shutdown, commonest), normal (mild or between attacks) or high (trabeculitis, PAS, pupil block, steroid response), so it must be measured at every visit.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.68 (Q447), key p.70 (explanation box)' }
